dex.cljc:649:;;    :branching-factor n                             ; every node the session folds or\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:791:      :branching-factor n              at least 2 (default 512)\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:801:  [{:keys [mode schema intake branching-factor ids ref-type]}]\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:807:  (validate-branching! (or branching-factor 512))\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:812:  (let [branching (or branching-factor 512)\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:815:                                {:branching-factor branching,\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:829:     :branching-factor branching,\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1179:                  :branching-factor (:branching-factor index-state)}\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1325:                                  {:branching-factor (:branching-factor\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1391:        branching (:branching-factor manifest)\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1393:                                {:branching-factor branching,\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1404:                              {:branching-factor branching, :ref-type :strong}),\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1406:     :branching-factor branching,\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:308:   publish-index! (e.g. :branching-factor, :select-stream). Publication is\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:593:              (integer? (:branching-factor m))))))\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:941:                          :branching-factor (:branching-factor manifest)\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:177:before appending a datagram. Defaults to <code>127.0.0.1</code> in loopback S3.\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:655:  iterative lookup asks again for more. S3 proves by test that the largest\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:805:Encoded message bound, send and reassembly. S3 measures real covered-index nodes\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:878:S3 measured a real `dao.space.index/publish-index!` build at the default\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:880:index node blobs and one manifest; the largest canonical value was 26,497\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:881:bytes, and its maximal-cookie `:store` wire value was 26,720 bytes. The\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:909:Deleted in S3, together with <code>dao.jing.dht.node</code>, which implements\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:923:Deleted in S3.\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:941:Rewritten in S2 and S3.\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:948:Order: **S1 and S2 -> S3 -> S4 -> S5.** S1 and S2 own disjoint files and neither\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:956:the same format, and does not touch `dao.jing`. S3, which joins the two,\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:981:`cookie-for`. Chunks are S3; the keyed MAC is S4. Acceptance:\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:1001:**S3: real sockets and chunks.** Depends on S1 and S2. Chunk records and\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:1019:**S4: hardening, lookup repair, storage bound.** Depends on S3. The keyed\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789691000000-review-dao-jing-cbor-glm.prompt.md:18:backend (memory, file, PostgreSQL, S3, remote/DHT). Jing is\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789691000000-review-dao-jing-cbor-glm.prompt.md:53:  third-party-implementable* backend layer (PostgreSQL, S3, the\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789691000000-review-dao-jing-cbor-glm.prompt.md:80:   backend, file backend, PostgreSQL/S3 backends — check whether carving\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789630022715-architect-jing-cbor-signoff.prompt.md:41:storage, like Datomic storage. Memory, files, PostgreSQL, S3, remote\\ntest/dao/jing_test.cljc:228:    (is (= \\\"552ee5466783e35ae00f007695e632684979d86c35487d5d9d062658f30b5994\\\"\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789680000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:25:- Backend/codec layering is otherwise clean — memory, PostgreSQL, S3 need zero CBOR knowledge (file backend's frame envelope is the one ambiguity, see finding 1).\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789700000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor-final.prompt.md:55:   PostgreSQL/S3 when they're equally in-repo.\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789569973938-review-jing-cbor-design.prompt.md:35:storage, like Datomic storage. Memory, files, PostgreSQL, S3, remote services,\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789569973938-review-jing-cbor-design.prompt.md:67:   concretely about memory, PostgreSQL, and S3 without requiring new backend\\ncollab/dao-jing/1788950986527-storage-space-transactor-phase1.glm-5.3.findings.md:17:   **Body unchanged** — S3, S6, S7 preserved verbatim, including all three\\ncollab/dao-jing/1788950986527-storage-space-transactor-phase1.glm-5.3.findings.md:36:   pinning S2, S3, S6, S7 directly through `index/datoms-from-elements`\\ncollab/dao-jing/1788950986527-storage-space-transactor-phase1.glm-5.3.findings.md:40:   - S3: the transaction record's exact shape — a good flatten, plus six\\ntest/dao/stream/transit_test.cljc:80:           (= \\\"552ee5466783e35ae00f007695e632684979d86c35487d5d9d062658f30b5994\\\"\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789630022715-architect-jing-cbor-signoff.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:28:- **Backend independence, the central invariant: holds.** The byte-store contract is opaque `(address, bytes) → :inserted | :present` and `(address, sentinel) → bytes | sentinel`. Memory, file, remote, DHT, PostgreSQL, and S3 are interchangeable at that boundary; none needs CBOR, Clojure values, or domain knowledge. No database semantics enter storage. The numeric carriers and their portable operations are representation-level, which `dao.jing.md` explicitly permits, and they are exported by the codec namespace, not the storage wrapper.\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789690000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:20:- The architecture diagram (`:38`) still lists `file` as a bare peer of `memory | PostgreSQL | S3 | remote | DHT` under one undifferentiated `backend:` line — no textual marker that `file` is categorically different from the others there.\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789690000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:22:- The correction's own dichotomy — \\\"pluggable, third-party-implementable\\\" (PostgreSQL, S3, **and the memory/remote/DHT byte-store implementations**) vs. \\\"Jing's own built-in\\\" (file) — misclassifies `dao.jing.mem`, `dao.jing.remote`, and `dao.jing.dht`. Those are exactly as much Jing's own in-repo code as `dao.jing.file` is (same `dao.jing.*` namespace family, same source tree); PostgreSQL and S3 are the only backends that are actually third-party/not-yet-built. Grouping memory/remote/DHT with PostgreSQL/S3 to justify singling out file is not an accurate use of \\\"third-party.\\\"\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789700000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor-final.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:27:- The new Objective text (`:24-40`) grounds the exception in re-ingestion-across-process-death rather than \\\"built-in vs. third-party,\\\" and explicitly corrects the misclassification I raised in r2 (memory/remote/DHT are equally in-repo, not equally \\\"third-party\\\" to unbuilt PostgreSQL/S3). This is a strictly better argument than what r1/r2 converged on.\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789700000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor-final.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:28:- It also resolves my own r2 residual note: `## Backend changes` (`:334-337`) now opens with an explicit pointer back to the Objective's carve-out before its `### Memory and files` subsection, so a reader hitting that section first no longer has to reconstruct the exception from memory. The bare `backend: memory | file | PostgreSQL | S3 | remote | DHT | ...` diagram (`:46`) is still undifferentiated, but it now sits *below* an Objective paragraph that already explains the exception in detail — a minor residual, not a contradiction.\\ncollab/dao-jing/storage-space-query-v2.glm-5.3.findings.md:59:(ownership), S (snapshot), E (evaluator), L (lazy published). R5 pins S1-S3\\ncollab/dao-jing/storage-space-query-v2.glm-5.3.findings.md:146:this path (the old R5 throw tests are replaced by S1-S3 by the plan\\ncollab/dao-jing/1789569973938-review-jing-cbor-design.glm-5.3.findings.md:35:- **Storage correctness (item 5) — carried over and strengthened.** Snapshot copying, CAS insert-if-absent, `:present` byte read-back, fsync-before-ack, torn-tail recovery all descend from the current verified backends; verification improves (byte hash vs re-print). Old-store rejection is achievable: complete old EDN frames never look torn (framing is shared), and EDN text fails CBOR frame decode loudly before any truncation — the required nondestructive-rejection test is already listed. PostgreSQL/S3 reasoning stays at the right altitude.\\ncollab/dao-jing/1788977648939-architect-jing-remote-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:255:| S3 | A bind failure throws from `serve-content!` and retains nothing | `[D]` **new** — `serving/start!` answers `transport-error` |\\ncollab/dao-jing/1788977648939-architect-jing-remote-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:899:   server on the first's port throws (S3); `(:stop! server)` twice returns\\ncollab/dao-jing/1788977648939-architect-jing-remote-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:1017:plus §5.2 #3–#9. They pin N1–N3, N6, N7, N10, N11, S2, S3, S5 through\\ntest/dao/stream/remote_channel_test.cljc:655:;; S3b: writer-surface tables, identities dials, detach, drain\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/class_prelude_test.cljc:203:;; ------------------------------------------------------------------- S3\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/object_parity_test.cljc:2:  \\\"Stage 0 (the T2 remainder), steps S3 and S4: constructibility by `:kind`\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/object_parity_test.cljc:114:;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ S3\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/object_parity_test.cljc:452:           (let [r (S3 {:js/str \\\"defineProperty\\\"} o {:js/str \\\"a\\\"} d)\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/object_parity_test.cljc:466:                   (S3 {:js/str \\\"defineProperty\\\"} o {:js/str \\\"a\\\"} d)\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/object_parity_test.cljc:468:                   (S3 {:js/str \\\"defineProperty\\\"} o {:js/str \\\"a\\\"} d))))\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/programs_p6.cljc:30:   arm), S3 (the dynamic-scope profile), S4 (namespaces, `use`, the\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/programs_p6.cljc:170:  \\\"Includes inside functions (P6 S3): the including scope is the function's\\ntest/yin/repl/dht_head_test.cljc:674:;; The channel owns the dial's liveness (H2 sign-off notes 1 to 3, S3a-2)\\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_board_test.cljc:4:   S3a-2).\\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_board_test.cljc:623:;; Explicit stop and stream-side liveness (S3a-2)\\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_follow_test.cljc:1087:;; :polled and :answered (S3a-2)\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:258:   S3's tracing (the trace cell and its hooks in the prelude) moves it\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:259:   once more. S3's shared-call prelude revision moves it with `py`.\\\"\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:244:   S3 shared-call publication moved the Python module manifest.\\\"\\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/frame_test.cljc:273:              [\\\"S3\\\" :window-representation (assoc worked-frame :yin.k/window {-1 nil}) worked-code :wait task]\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc:300:  \\\"What the guest sees of each module outcome (S3a, `py/int-result`)\\ntest/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc:2:  \\\"yin.vm.linker M4 slice S3 (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md sections 7.2 to\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_fixtures.cljc:43:   bits), and `small`, as the S3a limit tests run.\\\"\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:784:  (testing \\\"S3 arithmetic, power and shifts promote exactly on every host\\\"\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:1129:;; C3 slice S3a: integer limit reasons are guest exceptions\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_ops_test.cljc:2:  \\\"C3 S3-A/B/C exact operators and range/index audit on all four VMs.\\\"\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:45:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_22\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,205p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree/cursor.cljc; sed -n '70,101p' test/dao/data/btree_test.cljc; sed -n '870,891p' docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md; sed -n '74,122p' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc; sed -n '270,312p' src/cljc/dao/stream/datagram.cljc; rg -n 'max-message|bound|limit' src/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc | head -45\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(ns dao.data.btree.cursor\\n  \\\"A pure, stepped, ascending cursor over PERSISTED B-tree node blobs\\n   (docs/design Phase 4b seam; the seam spec r7 as amended by r8).\\n\\n   The cursor never reads. It is an immutable value that a driver advances:\\n\\n     (start descriptor comparator)  -> cursor      no I/O; PINS the comparator\\n     (step cursor)                  -> outcome     one local transition\\n     (supply cursor response)       -> cursor      hand over the requested node\\n\\n   Outcomes of `step` (exactly one):\\n\\n     {:status :continue  :cursor c}                local progress, step again\\n     {:status :need-node :address a :cursor c}     fetch ONE node, then `supply`\\n     {:status :item      :value k :cursor c}       one stored key, in order\\n     {:status :done      :cursor c}                terminal\\n\\n   The descriptor is {:root address-or-nil :count n :branching-factor b\\n   :lower nil-or-{:key k :inclusive? bool} :upper nil-or-{:key k\\n   :inclusive? bool}}. A nil bound is unbounded; {:key nil ...} is a genuine\\n   nil key bound. `start` does not retain the branching factor at all (it is\\n   accepted in the descriptor and ignored), and this namespace never rejects\\n   a node for occupancy, fill or key count (those are builder policy, not\\n   persisted-format rules).\\n\\n   The persisted shapes are those of `dao.data.btree/node->blob`:\\n   a leaf is {:keys [...]}; a branch is {:level n :keys [...] :addresses\\n   [...]} whose keys are CHILD MAXIMA. A response is {:address a :node blob}.\\n\\n   ---- Protocol -------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n   One request is pending at a time. Stepping a waiting cursor returns the\\n   same request and makes no progress (idempotent). `supply` throws a\\n   structured error (ex-data :dao.data.btree.cursor/error) when: no node is\\n   pending (:unexpected-response); the address differs from the pending one\\n   (:address-mismatch); or the request was already satisfied\\n   (:already-satisfied). Only the MOST RECENT satisfied address is remembered,\\n   so a stale response naming an earlier satisfied address, when nothing is\\n   pending, is classified :unexpected-response rather than :already-satisfied;\\n   every such path rejects, so the distinction is diagnostic only. A supplied node is validated by later `step`\\n   transitions, never inside `supply`; a malformed node is a structured\\n   failure (:malformed-node, :inconsistent-level, :unordered-keys,\\n   :separator-mismatch), never a silent empty or an endless traversal.\\n\\n   ---- Transitions (no multi-node recursive descent, no hidden loop) -------\\n\\n   begin -> request the root (an empty tree is :done with ZERO requests);\\n   validate (chunked) -> enter; enter a branch: choose a child (seek by child\\n   maxima, else leftmost), push an immutable path frame, request ONE child;\\n   enter a leaf: position (seek) and yield keys one at a time; an exhausted\\n   leaf ascends: each popped frame is one :continue, the next sibling is\\n   requested, and the descent is again one request per level; the upper bound\\n   stops before emitting a key outside it; no bounds is a full walk. Every\\n   transition does O(1) work bounded by constants: validation advances at\\n   most `validation-chunk` keys per step and a seek is a binary search over a\\n   node's keys (at most 32 comparisons).\\n\\n   Validation (the ONLY checks): leaf/branch shape; a branch has a positive\\n   integer level, vector keys and addresses of EQUAL length, every address\\n   present; keys strictly ordered under the pinned comparator; a child's level\\n   is strictly lower than its parent's (leaves are 0); a fetched child's\\n   maximum compares EQUAL to the parent separator under the pinned comparator\\n   (comparison equality, never content identity); a response matches the\\n   pending address. Yielded keys are also checked strictly increasing across\\n   leaves. That check is STRICT because of a safety premise: persisted trees\\n   are built from sorted sets, which cannot hold comparator-equal elements, so\\n   two comparator-equal consecutive keys are unrepresentable in this format\\n   (kind-tied values survive only through differing later comparator fields).\\n   A future editor must neither weaken the check to non-strict nor extend it\\n   to a format, such as multiset blobs, where comparator-equal neighbors are\\n   legal and it would falsely reject. Nothing else: roots, single-child spines and partial final chunks\\n   read fine. Validation is incremental and never fetches a sibling to\\n   validate the selected path; it does not certify the whole tree.\\n\\n   Path frames hold the immutable branch blob, its address, level, and the\\n   selected child position (which is also the advancement position). No frame\\n   holds a storage handle, execution state or clock; cursor state is a local\\n   value and is not promised to be serializable. This namespace calls nothing\\n   that restores implicitly (no resident-root, node-child, slice, seq on a\\n   set) and acquires no dependency on datoms, DaoSpace or any comparator.\\n\\n   ---- Comparator, sentinels and the stopping rule (r8) ---------------------\\n\\n   Bounds and node ordering use the comparator pinned at `start`; content\\n   equality must never terminate a comparator-equal range. Prefix scans are a\\n   caller policy, not inferred by the cursor: the caller seeks with a lower\\n   bound, takes keys while its own prefix test (for DaoSpace, `same-slot?`,\\n   a comparator tie on the scanned slot) holds, and stops requesting work at\\n   the first key outside the prefix. The scan must CONTINUE across values\\n   that compare equal but differ in content identity (1 and 1.0, 1.0M and\\n   1.00M, 0.0 and -0.0, which interleave by the later comparator fields);\\n   content-strict filtering happens AFTER candidate production. Never\\n   manufacture a generic upper key by replacing unspecified fields with nil:\\n   under nil-first ordering that is an incorrect upper boundary for a prefix\\n   range.\\n\\n   Seek keys are canonical datom slot positions [e a v t m], never\\n   physically reordered to match a comparator's field priority. Under the\\n   nil-first comparison of sentinel fields:\\n\\n     | Selected access path               | Lower sentinel       |\\n     |------------------------------------|----------------------|\\n     | EAVT, entity e                     | [e nil nil nil nil]  |\\n     | AEVT, attribute a                  | [nil a nil nil nil]  |\\n     | AVET, attribute a and value v      | [nil a v nil nil]    |\\n     | VAET, value v                      | [nil nil v nil nil]  |\\n     | Full EAVT walk                     | no lower bound       |\\n\\n   These are seek keys, not stored datoms. (This is usage documentation only:\\n   the namespace depends on none of it.)\\\")\\n\\n\\n(def validation-chunk\\n  \\\"The most keys one validating transition examines.\\\"\\n  32)\\n\\n\\n(defn- fail\\n  [kind message data]\\n  (throw (ex-info message (assoc data ::error kind))))\\n\\n\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n;; start\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n(defn start\\n  \\\"A new cursor over the tree described by `descriptor`, pinning\\n   `comparator` (a two-argument function returning a negative, zero or\\n   positive number) for its whole life. Performs no I/O.\\\"\\n  [descriptor comparator]\\n  (let [{:keys [root lower upper], cnt :count} descriptor]\\n    (when-not (fn? comparator)\\n      (fail :invalid-descriptor \\\"a comparator function is required\\\" {}))\\n    (when-not (and (integer? cnt) (not (neg? cnt)))\\n      (fail :invalid-descriptor \\\":count must be a nonnegative integer\\\" {}))\\n    (when (and (pos? cnt) (nil? root))\\n      (fail :invalid-descriptor \\\"a non-empty tree needs a root address\\\"\\n            {:count cnt}))\\n    (doseq [[k b] [[:lower lower] [:upper upper]]]\\n      (when-not (or (nil? b) (and (map? b) (contains? b :key)))\\n        (fail :invalid-descriptor \\\"a bound is nil or {:key k :inclusive? bool}\\\"\\n              {:bound k})))\\n    {::phase (if (or (zero? cnt) (nil? root)) :done :begin)\\n     ::cmp comparator\\n     ::root root\\n     ::lower lower\\n     ::upper upper\\n     ::seeking? (some? lower)\\n     ::path []\\n     ::leaf nil\\n     ::pending nil\\n     ::incoming nil\\n     ::vi 0\\n     ::shaped? false\\n     ::last-key nil\\n     ::has-last? false\\n     ::satisfied nil}))\\n\\n\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n;; supply\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n(defn supply\\n  \\\"Hand the requested node to a waiting cursor. Returns a cursor that the\\n   next `step` calls validate. Throws a structured error when nothing is\\n   pending, the address differs, or the request was already satisfied.\\\"\\n  [cursor response]\\n  (let [pending (::pending cursor)\\n        address (:address response)]\\n    (cond\\n      (not= :awaiting (::phase cursor))\\n      (if (and (some? (::satisfied cursor)) (= address (::satisfied cursor)))\\n        (fail :already-satisfied \\\"the request was already satisfied\\\"\\n              {:address address})\\n        (fail :unexpected-response \\\"no node is pending\\\" {:address address}))\\n\\n      (not= address (:address pending))\\n      (fail :address-mismatch \\\"response does not match the pending address\\\"\\n            {:expected (:address pending), :actual address})\\n\\n      :else\\n      (assoc cursor\\n             ::phase :validate\\n             ::pending nil\\n             ::incoming {:address address, :blob (:node response)}\\n             ::vi 0\\n             ::shaped? false\\n             ::satisfied address))))\\n\\n\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n;; helpers\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n(defn- first-ge\\n  \\\"First index in keys whose key compares >= k; (count keys) when none.\\n   A binary search: at most 32 comparisons.\\\"\\n  [cmp ks k]\\n  (loop [low 0\\n         high (count ks)]\\n    (if (< low high)\\n      (let [mid (quot (+ low high) 2)]\\n        (if (neg? (cmp (nth ks mid) k))\\n                 (dec i)))\\n        v))))\\n\\n\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n;; Invariant walker\\n\\n(defn- check-node\\n  \\\"Validates the subtree rooted at node; returns {:count n :depth d}.\\\"\\n  [node cmp bf root?]\\n  (let [min-fill (quot bf 2)\\n        ks (bt/node-keys-vec node)\\n        len (count ks)]\\n    (is (<= len bf) \\\"node exceeds branching factor\\\")\\n    (is (every? #(neg? (cmp (ks %) (ks (inc %)))) (range (dec len)))\\n        \\\"keys not strictly ascending within node\\\")\\n    (if (bt/branch? node)\\n      (do (is (>= len (if root? 2 min-fill)) \\\"branch underfilled\\\")\\n          (is (every? #(zero? (cmp (ks %)\\n                                   (bt/node-lim-key (bt/node-child node %))))\\n                      (range len))\\n              \\\"branch key does not equal child's max key\\\")\\n          (let [subs (mapv #(check-node (bt/node-child node %) cmp bf false)\\n                           (range len))]\\n            (is (apply = (map :depth subs)) \\\"leaves at differing depths\\\")\\n            {:count (reduce + (map :count subs)),\\n             :depth (inc (:depth (first subs)))}))\\n      (do (when-not root? (is (>= len min-fill) \\\"leaf underfilled\\\"))\\n          {:count len, :depth 0}))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- check-set\\n<code>127.0.0.1</code>\\n</td>\\n<td>\\nMust match the composed socket's bind family.\\n</td>\\n</tr>\\n</table>\\n\\nS3 measured a real `dao.space.index/publish-index!` build at the default\\nbranching factor of 512 over 512 distinct datoms. It emitted two covered\\nindex node blobs and one manifest; the largest canonical value was 26,497\\nbytes, and its maximal-cookie `:store` wire value was 26,720 bytes. The\\n65,536-byte default therefore covers this measured build. Larger application\\nvalues still require an explicit composition limit or refusal.\\n\\nEvery stream the DHT is composed over is an evicting ring: requests,\\nanswers, facts, raw traffic. None is a complete-history log.\\n\\n## 10. Today's code, and the slices that replace it\\n\\nWhat the tree holds on 2026-09-30, all of it replaced by this epic with no\\ncompatibility path:\\n\\n\\n(defn sha256\\n  \\\"SHA-256 hex digest of the UTF-8 bytes of string s. Rejection on\\n   unpaired surrogates via dao.host.text/utf8-encode.\\\"\\n  [s]\\n  (bytes->hex (hash/sha256-bytes (text/utf8-encode s))))\\n\\n\\n(defn sha256-bytes\\n  \\\"SHA-256 hex digest of host bytes bs (byte[] on the JVM, Uint8Array on\\n   ClojureScript, Uint8List on Dart). `(sha256-bytes (canonical-bytes v))`\\n   is the digest for `(content-hash v {:algorithm :sha256})`.\\\"\\n  [bs]\\n  (bytes->hex (hash/sha256-bytes bs)))\\n\\n\\n(def default-hash-algorithm\\n  \\\"The default algorithm used for implicit minting (content-hash, segment-key,\\n   materialize!). Never consulted when verifying an existing address.\\\"\\n  :blake3)\\n\\n\\n(def registry\\n  \\\"The closed, immutable algorithm registry. Both initial entries produce\\n   32-byte (64 lowercase hex character) digests.\\\"\\n  {:blake3 {:address-id \\\"blake3\\\", :digest-bytes 32}\\n   :sha256 {:address-id \\\"sha256\\\", :digest-bytes 32}})\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private registry-by-address-id\\n  (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(:address-id v) k])) registry))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private algorithm-id-pattern\\n  \\\"Algorithm address identifiers are lowercase ASCII alphanumeric strings.\\\"\\n  #\\\"[a-z0-9]+\\\")\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private hex-digit-pattern\\n  #\\\"[0-9a-f]+\\\")\\n\\n\\n(defn blake3-bytes\\n  \\\"BLAKE3 lowercase hex digest of host bytes bs (byte[] on the JVM,\\n   Uint8Array on ClojureScript, Uint8List on Dart).\\\"\\n  [bs]\\n  (bytes->hex (hash/blake3-bytes bs)))\\n\\n    [_]\\n    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\\n     :dao.stream/descriptor the-descriptor\\n     :dao.stream/identity (:dao.stream/identity the-descriptor)})\\n\\n\\n  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\\n\\n  (append!\\n    [_ v]\\n    (if @closed\\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}\\n      (let [checked (check-outbound v max-bytes)]\\n        (if-some [reason (:refusal checked)]\\n          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-value\\n           :dao.stream.datagram/reason reason}\\n          (let [destination (:dao.stream.datagram/destination v)\\n                r (try\\n                    (send-fn (:dao.stream.datagram/host destination)\\n                             (:dao.stream.datagram/port destination)\\n                             (:bytes checked))\\n                    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\\n                      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\\n                       :dao.stream.datagram/reason (str e)}))]\\n            (if (and (map? r) (contains? r :dao.stream/outcome))\\n              (if (#{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/transport-error\\n                     :dao.stream/closed} (:dao.stream/outcome r))\\n                r\\n                {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error})\\n              {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))))))\\n\\n\\n  stream/IDaoStreamClosable\\n\\n  (close!\\n    [_]\\n    (vreset! closed true)\\n    (try (close-fn)\\n         (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\\n           nil))\\n    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))\\n\\n\\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:2:  \\\"In-memory, unbounded, append-only DaoStream v2 transport: the\\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:13:   (no capacity is declared; the transport is logically unbounded and\\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:156:    ;; after it.  Unbounded means conj is the whole write path — no\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:2:  \\\"The explicit Jing/transport boundary adapter (D7's condition, U10).\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:18:   byte string that crossed the wrong lane fails loudly at the boundary\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:72:   Transit unwrap is a payload that crossed the wrong boundary.\\\"\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:95:   boundary operation itself. There is no unwrap-payload: the canonical\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/dao-stream/1794057236000*; tail -n 50 collab/dao-space/1794057586000*.stdout.log; rg -n 'byte.stream|chunk|R[1-7]|Completed|OVER|REJECT|APPROV' collab/dao-space/1794057158000*; rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\(def|branching|publish-opts|Settings|recording' src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/dao-stream/1794057236000*; tail -n 50 collab/dao-space/1794057586000*.stdout.log; rg -n 'byte.stream|chunk|R[1-7]|Completed|OVER|REJECT|APPROV' collab/dao-space/1794057158000*; rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\(def|branching|publish-opts|Settings|recording' src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-07 12:24:18 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-07 19:24:18 +07\nCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode / glm-5.3-flash, master-green orchestrator seat)\nSession-ID: not-applicable (interactive seat)\n\n# OWNER ADDENDUM: the message-bound problem is GENERAL to every dao.stream implementation\n\n## The owner's words (2026-11-07, during the byte-cut design review, verbatim)\n\n> \"but this problem is a general problem that all dao.stream implementation faces\"\n\n## What this changes\n\nThe first architect verdict (collab/dao-space/1794057586000-architect-byte-cut-\ndesign-review.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log, APPROVED-WITH-CHANGES R1-R7) scoped\nbyte-sized cutting to the INDEX BUILDER (dao.space.index) as the consumer that\nhappens to carry large datoms. The owner's generalization relocates the design\nquestion: EVERY dao.stream implementation composes messages that must respect\nthe transport bound (dao.jing.dht ::max-message-bytes 65536, refused\nfail-closed at send), and each one currently re-derives that discipline\nprivately (or discovers the bound by refusal, as the nightly did).\n\nRequired consequence for the reconciliation round (next architect step, after\nthe gpt-6-astra audit lands): adjudicate the LOCUS of the fix —\n\n(a) index-builder-local (what R1-R7 currently specify): only the index store\n    cuts by bytes; every other dao.stream consumer keeps its own discipline;\n(b) dao.stream-layer: the bound becomes a first-class stream/transport\n    contract — e.g. the composition exposes its message budget as a queryable\n    property, and/or dao.stream provides standardized segmentation (a\n    chunked-message-sequence protocol) so oversized logical payloads are cut\n    into bounded messages ONCE, at the stream layer, with reassembly\n    semantics every peer already implements;\n(c) some split: transport exposes the budget + refuses overbound (already\n    true); stream layer offers optional segmentation; consumers like the\n    index builder still self-cut because their payload has internal structure\n    (sorted pages) that only they can split semantically.\n\nPoint (c) is the likely landing zone — the index page cut is semantic\n(the datom order is the index; only the builder knows legal cut points),\nwhile the bound knowledge should come FROM the composition, not be hardcoded\n(R5 already requires deriving the budget from max-message-bytes; the\ngeneralization says every consumer must derive it the same way). But that is\nthe architects' call, not this seat's.\n\n## Status of in-flight work\n\ngpt-6-astra's audit of R1-R7 is in flight (read-only; dispatched before this\naddendum — it will NOT have seen this file). Its verdict, plus this addendum,\ngo to the reconciliation step before any engineer re-brief. No engineer\ndispatch happens until the locus question is adjudicated.\n\n— the master-green orchestrator seat\n- The verified-frontier gate compares the manifest field to `:publish-opts` for equality before reusing a suffix frontier ([yin/repl/index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:368)). An emergent value varies per publication, so the gate would fail almost every round and force full reconstruction — or, if “fixed” by deleting the comparison, would reuse frontiers without the check that validates them. Do not remove that comparison.\n- One field cannot describe four trees. Byte cuts give EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET different leaf counts over the same datoms. There is no single emergent count to record.\n- The manifest is a hash input (`jing/segment-key manifest`, [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:619)). Redefining the field churns every manifest address and defeats the cache-sharing the directive wants to preserve, for scalar trees that never had the problem.\n\nRequired (R3): keep the dual constraint and the schema. Cut a page when either the byte budget or the count cap (512) is reached. `:branching-factor` keeps meaning “the count cap this tree was built and must be restored with”; the manifest stays exactly `{:indexes :count :branching-factor}`. If the budget must later be recorded for restore fidelity, that is a schema proposal to the work log per the directive’s own rule — not this slice, and not a reason to overload the existing field.\n\n## 5. Reverting `cb2cbe11` (`:repair-slots 48`) — REJECTED as a step of this change\n\nThe brief’s hedge (“verify rather than assume”) is right; its presumption is wrong, and its premise — “the 8× amplification collapses” — does not hold. At a 48 KiB budget, code-heavy REPL pages hold roughly the same datom counts as fix 5’s 64-key pages, so per-round address counts stay in fix 5’s 16–34 range, not the 512-era 9–16. The directive itself already says this correctly: page count per round still scales with volume, so the cadence question remains open.\n\nThe live evidence also already superseded the brief: `/tmp/nrf-branch-wt` carries an uncommitted sweep replacing 48 with a measured 40 (49 idle ticks in a 400-tick observation, against 32 at 48 and 3 at 32; queue peaks 221 at 16 slots, 133 at 24, 81 at 32). A blanket `git revert cb2cbe11` returns the store to the `dao.space.dht` default of 16 — the configuration measured never to drain.\n\nRequired (R4): no revert in the byte-cut change. After byte-cut lands, re-run the dart ticker-idle test at 16, 40, and 48 with byte-cut pages and pin the measured winner. Retire fix 5’s `:branching-factor 64` override in the same change (R3 makes it redundant); keep its commit in history, as the brief says.\n\n## 6. Sequencing — implement now on `nightly-reds-fix` (owner redirect upheld)\n\nThe directive’s “after master-green, do not fold into nightly-reds-fix” was written before the owner halted the slot sweep as solving the wrong problem. Merging the branch as-is would land a known-wrong workaround (64-key pages plus slot tuning fitted to them) and then immediately rework it. The branch is also the only place the failing configuration reproduces. Implement byte-cut there, with independent review before merge.\n\nRisks owned with this pick: the change touches bulk-build behavior on a nightly-fix branch, so the merge waits on the brief’s full dart + node matrix plus the index namespaces; pin churn (see Missed) must be reported as measured emergent counts, never fitted. Worktree hygiene needs care first: `/tmp/nrf-branch-wt` is detached at `4f772d61` with the uncommitted 40-slot edit, while `nightly-reds-fix` names `cb2cbe11`. Reconcile that state explicitly before any implementation commit; do not let the slot edit ride along silently.\n\nThe alternative’s risk is worse: master-green merges fix 5, the repair cadence gets tuned to the wrong page shape a second time, and the byte work starts over on a new slice.\n\n## 7. The 48 KiB budget — DEMAND MEASUREMENT; number not confirmed\n\n48 KiB is safe but unjustified, and as specified it is wired wrong:\n\n- It hardcodes 49,152 in `dao.space.index`, while the bound it derives from is per-composition data (`::max-message-bytes` in [dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:73), overridable at composition). Raise the composition bound and the index still over-cuts; lower it and the index overflows. Required (R5): derive the budget at the publish boundary — budget = composition `max-message-bytes` − measured `:store` envelope − margin — and pass it through `:publish-opts`. The envelope oracle already exists: `store-message-size` with maximal `q` and full cookie ([dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:228)). Tests must assert against the full store message, not the leaf alone.\n- The headroom is far larger than the envelope needs. The only measurement on record shows 223 bytes of envelope (26,497 → 26,720). Reserving 16 KiB inflates page count by roughly a third versus a ~60 KiB budget, which directly worsens the repair economics in §5. But S3 measured small datoms only; no REPL code-datom leaf distribution has been measured at any budget.\n- Required (R6): before pinning a number, measure real REPL covered-index leaves (the dart `yin.repl.dht-test` tree) — leaf byte distribution, emergent counts per index, per-round address counts. Then set the budget from the measured envelope plus an stated margin. 49,152 may stand as a provisional conservative pin only if the brief says that is what it is.\n\n## What both artifacts missed entirely\n\n1. Branch sizing is undercounted in the brief. A branch blob is `{:level n :keys [separator datoms] :addresses [...]}` ([btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:1080)). “Each entry’s bytes = its canonical address encoding” omits the separator keys — full datoms, the largest part — plus map/array framing. Branch cuts must account the whole blob with the R1 summation method, or branches overflow after leaves are fixed.\n2. Bulk fill invariants. `split-ranges` builds chunks between `min-bf` and `max` aiming at the average ([btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:1998)), and branch levels group child nodes by the same count ranges. Byte grouping replaces this wholesale. The design must state that byte-cut leaves may be underfull by count, that this is legal for published immutable trees, and how a restored underfull leaf then behaves under `conj`/`disj` borrow/merge at `min-bf = bf/2`.\n3. Oversize publication semantics. `publish-index!` appends blobs to the intake before the DHT ever refuses them; an over-budget leaf therefore yields a locally durable, remotely unacknowledged manifest — the original symptom in quieter form. The design must surface that as a publication failure at publish time (R2’s emission check does), not leave it to ack collapse.\n4. Budget threading for the incremental session. The REPL indexer’s `:publish-opts` reach only the full-reconstruction path; the suffix path’s session is rebuilt by `index/restore` from the manifest. Unless the budget is threaded through `restore`/`session` and the storage Settings’ composition, the two paths will silently use different budgets after a restart.\n5. Test pins beyond the four shape pins. Grep shows the load-bearing ones: the default-factor pin `(= 512 (:branching-factor manifest))` and the many `{:branching-factor 4}` fixtures in [index_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/index_test.cljc:163), the manifest-shape assertions at [index_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/index_test.cljc:573), and the DHT test’s `(= 64 … :branching-factor)` pin added by fix 5. Under R3 the 512/4 semantics survive; only the fix-5 pin and any realized-count assertions change, each reported with its measured value per measure-then-pin.\n6. The manifest blob itself and non-DHT intake. Pages also travel intake streams and local stores that have no 64 KiB bound. Byte cutting is harmless there, but the design should say the budget is a DHT-derived composition input (R5), not a new property of the index format — otherwise a future non-DHT composition inherits an unexplained page shape.\n\n## Required changes for the re-brief\n\n- R1: encode-once length summation for leaf and branch cuts; exact header accounting; no candidate re-encodes (§2).\n- R2: emission-bound check plus bulk-rebuild fallback on the incremental `publish!` path; budget threaded through `session`/`restore` (§3, Missed 4).\n- R3: dual constraint (byte budget and 512 count cap); `:branching-factor` semantics and manifest schema unchanged; retire only the fix-5 `64` override (§4, §5).\n- R4: no `cb2cbe11` revert in this change; slot count re-measured after landing (§5).\n- R5: budget derived from the composition’s `max-message-bytes` minus the `store-message-size` envelope, passed via `:publish-opts`; tests assert full store-message size (§7).\n- R6: REPL leaf measurement precedes pinning the budget number; 49,152 provisional only (§7).\n- R7: sequence on `nightly-reds-fix` now, after reconciling the detached worktree and its uncommitted 40-slot edit; full dart + node matrix before merge (§6).\n\nWith R1–R7 folded in, this is approved to implement.\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.prompt.md:17:3. collab/dao-space/1794057586000-architect-byte-cut-design-review.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log — the FIRST architect's verdict: APPROVED-WITH-CHANGES, required changes R1-R7\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.prompt.md:23:1. Verify each of R1-R7 independently: is it correct, complete, and minimal? Flag any that are wrong, overreaching, or missing code-level constraints the first reviewer missed.\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.prompt.md:24:2. Challenge the first review's key claims: (a) the O(n) encode-once length-summation is exact because CBOR concatenation is additive apart from headers — verify this against cbor.cljc's actual encoding (map framing, identifier bytes, header width thresholds); (b) keeping :branching-factor as the count cap (R3) is sound given every consumer (validator, restored-indexes Settings threading, verify-candidate, frontier gate at yin/repl/index.cljc:368, manifest-as-hash-input); (c) the incremental publish! path can enforce the budget at blob emission with zero extra encodes via the recording handle's :order of [address bs] (index.cljc:493).\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.prompt.md:25:3. Answer what the first review left open: the concrete R6 measurement protocol for the REPL leaf byte distribution; whether underfull leaves under byte-cut interact badly with min-bf borrow/merge on restore+conj; whether the empty-tree/single-leaf manifest cases break anything under the dual constraint.\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.prompt.md:31:Completed-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.prompt.md:32:Completed-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.prompt.md:47:   dao.stream's chunked-datagram framing), where should the bound contract\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.prompt.md:48:   live? Options (a) index-builder-local only (R1-R7 as written), (b)\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.prompt.md:52:   general contract would look like if (b)/(c), and how it changes R5's\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:3:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ll read the three artifacts in order, then audit R1–R7 and the stream-layer addendum against the code. I’ll keep this read-only and won’t run tests.\"}}\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/dao-space/1794056541000-orchestrator-byte-sized-index-pages.directive.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-11-07 12:09:01 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-07 19:09:01 +07\\nCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode / glm-5.3-flash, master-green orchestrator seat)\\nSession-ID: not-applicable (interactive seat)\\n\\n# OWNER DIRECTIVE: index pages are cut by encoded byte size, not by datom count\\n\\n## The owner's words (2026-11-07, in conversation during the repair-slot incident triage)\\n\\n> \\\"it seems to me this problem should be reframed in terms of byte size not in\\n> terms of datom count\\\"\\n\\n(Owner instruction relayed verbatim; the design below is this seat's working-out\\nof it, to be confirmed by an Architect round before implementation.)\\n\\n## Problem this solves\\n\\n`dao.space.index/from-sequential` cuts index pages by a fixed key count\\n(`:branching-factor`, generic default 512). The 64 KiB DHT message bound is a\\nBYTE bound on segment+envelope. A count-based cut cannot know whether 512 keys\\nencode to 3 KiB (small scalar datoms — safe) or 70 KiB (REPL code-datom pages —\\nrefused at the sender; the nightly 20261106-0944 ack collapse). Fix 5\\n(`4f772d61`, `:publish-opts {:branching-factor 64}` in yin/repl/dht.cljc) is a\\nper-store constant workaround; its 8× page amplification spawned the repair-slot\\neconomics incident (dart ticker-idle regression, currently under an engineer\\nsweep). A count dial cannot converge across lanes because the real constraint\\nwas never the count.\\n\\n## The design (working-out; Architect adjudication required)\\n\\n1. **Cut by encoded bytes.** `from-sequential` accumulates sorted datoms into a\\n   leaf until the next datom would push the leaf's canonical CBOR size past a\\n   byte budget, then cuts. Key count becomes emergent (64 for code-heavy trees,\\n   512+ for scalar ones). The manifest's `:branching-factor` field becomes\\n   descriptive (or is retired per the manifest schema's evolution rules —\\n   schema change is a proposal to the work log, not a unilateral edit).\\n2. **Budget derivation.** One authoritative constant: a page budget as a\\n   fraction of `dao.jing.dht`'s `max-message-bytes` (65536), headroom reserved\\n   for the envelope (chunk framing, cookie/pad fields). Proposed starting point:\\n   budget = 48 KiB (≈75% of the bound), confirmed by measurement per the\\n   existing \\\"S3 measures real covered-index nodes\\\" plan in dao.jing.dht.md:805.\\n3. **Branch pages** use the same byte budget over child-address entries.\\n4. **Deprecate count-based cuts** once byte-cut lands: fix 5's\\n   `:branching-factor 64` override and the generic 512 become defaults only for\\n   tests that pin tree *shape*, not for safety.\\n5. **Consequences to preserve/verify:**\\n   - determinism: the cut must remain a pure function of the sorted datom\\n     stream (canonical CBOR sizes are deterministic; sorting is stable) —\\n     addresses stay content-functions, so cache-sharing across rounds and the\\n     manifest-address provenance property survive.\\n   - the four `*_test` pins on page shape (dao.space index tests) get updated\\n     per the measure-then-pin rule, never reflexively.\\n   - the repair-slot tuning (current incident) becomes a cadence question only:\\n     byte-cut pages make per-page message size constant, but page COUNT per\\n     round still scales with datom volume, so `max-repairing` economics remain\\n     — the sweep's data still informs the schedule.\\n\\n## Sequencing (orchestrator's proposal)\\n\\n- NOW: finish the repair-slot sweep + branch blockers on nightly-reds-fix as\\n  dispatched (the merge needs a working 64-cut configuration regardless).\\n- AFTER master-green: an Architect round adjudicates this design; an\\n  implementation round follows on its own slice with its own pins. Do NOT\\n  fold it into nightly-reds-fix.\\n\\n— the master-green orchestrator seat\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:7:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/dao-space/1794056711000-engineer-byte-sized-index-cut.prompt.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-11-07 12:11:51 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-07 19:11:51 +07\\nCoding-Agent: qwen\\nSession-ID: b0bcaae8-80a2-4444-b36b-43ebe7d18f0b\\n\\n# Task: implement byte-sized index page cuts (owner directive; supersedes the repair-slot sweep)\\n\\nRole: Engineer (implementation + verification)\\n\\nImplementers:\\n- Model: qwen3.8-max | Assigned: 2026-11-07 19:11:51 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: continuation of the master-green engineering lane; the owner redirected the repair-slot sweep to this as the actual fix\\n\\n## Governing directive (READ FIRST, in full)\\n\\ncollab/dao-space/1794056541000-orchestrator-byte-sized-index-pages.directive.md\\n— the owner's reframe: \\\"this problem should be reframed in terms of byte size\\nnot in terms of datom count\\\" (2026-11-07). The Architect adjudication it\\nschedules is REPLACED BY review-after-implementation on this round (owner\\nredirect, same conversation): implement now on the branch, independent review\\nbefore merge per workflow step 7.\\n\\n## What to implement\\n\\nIn src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc (`from-sequential` and the publish path):\\n\\n1. **Byte-budgeted leaf cut.** Accumulate sorted datoms into a leaf; before\\n   appending the next datom, encode (canonical CBOR, `jing/cbor/encode`) the\\n   candidate leaf; if adding the datom would push the leaf's encoded size past\\n   a page budget, cut first. Budget: a new `dao.space.index` constant,\\n   default = 49152 bytes (48 KiB = 75% of dao.jing.dht's 65536\\n   `max-message-bytes`, headroom for envelope/framing), overridable per\\n   publish-opts for tests. Key count is emergent; no count cap is imposed\\n   (a single datom larger than the budget still forms a one-datom leaf — the\\n   store-level oversize refusal remains the last resort, unchanged).\\n2. **Byte-budgeted branch cut** with the same budget over child-address\\n   entries (each entry's bytes = its canonical address encoding).\\n3. **Manifest compatibility.** `:branching-factor` in the manifest becomes the\\n   EMERGENT count of the root's children if branch-cut was byte-driven — check\\n   the conforming-manifest validator (index.cljc:287-296) and every\\n   `:branching-factor` consumer (yin/repl/index.cljc:368-371 compares it) and\\n   reconcile: either keep the field as the realized branch count (preferred —\\n   no schema change) or remove the comparison; do not invent a schema change.\\n4. **Revert the workaround that this replaces**, on its own commit:\\n   `git revert cb2cbe11` (the `:repair-slots 48` commit) — with byte-sized\\n   pages the 8× address amplification collapses and the slot change's node\\n   breakage is moot; verify rather than assume (the dart/node matrix below\\n   decides; if evidence shows 48 slots is still needed with byte-cut pages,\\n   do not revert and report why).\\n   Also retire the `:publish-opts {:branching-factor 64}` override in\\n   src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc (fix 5's per-store constant): pages now self-size.\\n   Keep fix 5's INTENT (pages within the message bound) — enforced now by the\\n   builder. Fix 5's commit itself stays (history is not rewritten; this is a\\n   forward change on the branch).\\n5. **Pins:** dao.space index shape tests and any test pinning `:branching-factor`\\n   512/64 get updated per measure-then-pin (run them, report the new emergent\\n   counts, update the pins to the new realized values with a comment linking\\n   this directive). NEVER weaken an invariant to fit a byte budget.\\n\\n## Where\\n\\nWork in the git worktree /tmp/nrf-branch-wt (branch nightly-reds-fix @\\ncb2cbe11 + untracked scratch). Files in scope: src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc,\\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc, tests under test/dao/space/ and test/yin/repl/,\\nplus any test pinning page shape. Nothing else; no commits; no pushes; do not\\ntouch collab/ or other seats' untracked files.\\n\\n## Verification (the full matrix, per the scoped ruling)\\n\\n- dart (laptop, owner-authorized): cd /tmp/nrf-branch-wt && eval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\" && DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only yin.repl.dht-test  → all green\\n- dart: same, --only dao.space.query.datomic-test or the dao.space index namespace(s) covering the builder (find via grep) → all green\\n- node (shiva): cd ~/ci/datomworld-nightly-node; fetch origin nightly-reds-fix IS NOT ENOUGH — your diff is uncommitted, so apply it: git diff in /tmp/nrf-branch-wt > /tmp/bytecut.patch, scp to shiva, git apply in the clone (it is at cb2cbe11), then eval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\" && clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test --config-merge \\\"{:autorun false}\\\" && DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 node target/node-tests.js --test=yin.repl.dht-test/rounds-larger-than-the-pending-write-bound-are-acknowledged-never-busy,yin.repl.dht-test/with-peers-that-never-accept-twenty-rounds-report-and-the-ticker-idles,yin.repl.main-test/the-interactive-composition-holds-every-standard-language → all green\\n- JVM single namespaces are the QA seat's surface — do NOT run the JVM dht namespace; report the change so the QA seat re-verifies.\\n\\nBegin the final response exactly with:\\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\\n\\nReport: the cut algorithm as implemented (where the budget check sits), the\\nemergent page counts observed for the REPL test tree, every pin you changed and\\nwhy, the revert decision with evidence, git diff --stat, and BOTH verification\\noutputs verbatim (dart + node). Do not claim runs that did not occur.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:9:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/dao-space/1794057586000-architect-byte-cut-design-review.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"muse: workspace root: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld (cwd default)\\nmuse: workspace trust: trusted source=run-flag\\nmuse: warning: rules file at /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/CLAUDE.md is ignored this session because AGENTS.md takes precedence in that directory; merge still-applicable guidance into AGENTS.md or remove one of the two files\\nmuse: Skills: 39 loaded · 1 warning\\nmuse:   `skills_catalog` was trimmed to fit its size limit (32 KB): 2 descriptions compacted, 0 skill ids omitted\\nmuse:   Run `muse skills list --source all` for full diagnostics.\\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-07 13:09:31 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-07 20:09:31 +07\\n\\n# Architect review — byte-sized index page cuts\\n\\nScope read, read-only, no tests run: both artifacts in full; [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:286), [btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:1998), [storage.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc:93), [cbor.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:913), [jing.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:153), [yin/repl/index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:356), [dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:73), [dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:798), branch commits `4f772d61` and `cb2cbe11`, and the uncommitted sweep state in `/tmp/nrf-branch-wt`.\\n\\n## Overall disposition: APPROVED-WITH-CHANGES\\n\\nThe reframe is approved. The brief as written is not implementable: its sizing algorithm, its branch accounting, its manifest proposal, and its revert step each fail against the code. Required changes R1–R7 below are precise enough to re-brief from. Do not implement the brief verbatim.\\n\\n## 1. Framing — APPROVED, with one correction\\n\\nByte-sized cutting at the index builder is the correct axis. The bound that bites is a byte bound on the encoded `:store` message ([dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:228): `store-message-size`, refused in `send-datagram!` at [dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:254)). No count cap can respect it, because datom values are unbounded in size. Fix 5 (`4f772d61`, `:branching-factor 64`) only moves the failure to larger code values.\\n\\nThe alternatives are worse, and the review considered them:\\n\\n- Raising `max-message-bytes` alone: the design doc already marks it composition data to “confirm or raise” ([dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:805)), and S3 confirmed 65,536 covers only a small-datom build (26,497-byte canonical value, 26,720-byte wire value). Raising the bound does not bound a count-cut page; the same bug recurs at larger values, with larger reassembly state (`max-partial-messages` 64).\\n- DHT chunking: already exists for messages over `max-datagram` (1200) and is explicitly capped by `max-message-bytes` before splitting. It cannot carry an over-bound store message.\\n- Oversize-value chunking at another layer: a datom is the atomic index key in all four orders (`eavt-cmp`/`aevt-cmp`/`avet-cmp`/`vaet-cmp`). Splitting a value across leaves breaks ordering, lookup, and set semantics. Wrong layer.\\n\\nCorrection to the directive: “fixed max-message-bound-derived cut” is not an alternative to byte cutting; it is byte cutting. The owner’s framing stands.\\n\\n## 2. Cut algorithm — REJECTED as specified; replacement specified\\n\\nDeterminism: preserved either way. Canonical CBOR is a pure function of the value ([cbor.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:913) `encode`; [jing.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:153) `canonical-bytes`), and `from-sequential` sorts and dedupes before building ([btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:2059)). A size derived from those bytes is a pure function of the sorted stream.\\n\\nCost: encode-candidate-per-datom is O(k²·s) per leaf — each of k datoms re-encodes the whole accumulated leaf. At REPL page sizes this is the dominant build cost and heavy allocation churn on JS/Dart. Unacceptable when an exact O(n) accounting exists.\\n\\nRequired replacement (R1): encode once, sum lengths.\\n\\n- Encode each sorted datom once; keep its byte length.\\n- Leaf blob is `{:keys [d…]}` ([btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:1080) `node->blob`). Its encoded size is exactly: map framing + `:keys` identifier bytes + array header for n + Σ datom lengths. Recompute only the array header as n crosses CBOR header thresholds (24, 256, 65536). Cut when adding the next datom would exceed the budget. No candidate re-encodes.\\n- This is exact, not an estimate, because CBOR concatenation is additive apart from the header. State that proof in the design; forbid `pr-str` lengths or host string lengths anywhere in the accounting. Byte lengths come from the host byte array only.\\n- Keep the brief’s oversize rule: a single datom over budget still forms a one-datom leaf, and the store refusal remains the last resort. Add a test that pins this as a loud refusal, not a silent unacknowledged publication (see Missed, below).\\n\\n## 3. Bulk vs incremental — FAILS as briefed; design addition required\\n\\nThe brief heads its work “`from-sequential` and the publish path,” then specifies only the bulk builder. The two paths are different code:\\n\\n- Bulk: [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:564) `publish-index!` → `bt/from-sequential` with `{:branching-factor branching}`.\\n- Incremental: `session`/`fold-batch` folds with `bt/conj` ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1034)), and [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1142) `publish!` serializes the existing tree via `bt/store-tree`. Splits there obey only `Settings.branching-factor` (`bf*`/`min-bf*`, [btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:75)). The REPL uses both: full reconstruction via `transactor/publish!`, suffix publication via `index/publish!` ([yin/repl/index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:394)).\\n\\nSo a bulk-only byte cut leaves incremental publications able to emit the same 70 KiB leaf and collapse the same way. That alone would reject the brief.\\n\\nOne premise in the review prompt needs correcting: differently-shaped trees from the two paths do not break an invariant. The design already disavows cross-path manifest equality — bulk and `conj` partition the same rows differently, and acceptance is logical equality ([dao.space.index.as-observer.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md:685), item A9). Determinism is per-path: same sorted stream plus same budget gives same tree, on each path separately.\\n\\nRequired addition (R2): enforce the bound at blob emission on both paths, without redesigning the B-tree in this slice.\\n\\n- Keep `Settings` count-based. Do not make `node-conj`/`node-disj` byte-aware here; that rewrites split/merge/borrow invariants and is a separate design.\\n- Bulk path: byte-aware grouping in `from-sorted-array`/`from-sequential` per R1.\\n- Incremental path: `publish!` already holds every emitted blob’s canonical bytes in the recording handle (`:order` of `[address bs]`, [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:493)). Check each `bs` length against the budget there — zero extra encodes. On violation, rebuild that index from `bt/seq` through the byte-cut bulk builder and store that instead. Never emit an over-budget blob and never count such a publication as published.\\n- Thread the budget through `session`, `restore`, and the REPL `:publish-opts` so both paths see the same number.\\n\\n## 4. Manifest compatibility — brief’s preferred option REJECTED\\n\\n“Keep `:branching-factor` as the realized emergent count, no schema change” changes the field’s meaning while every consumer still reads the old one:\\n\\n- Validator requires an integer ≥ 2 ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:286)). An emergent root fanout is 0 for an empty tree and below 2 for a single-leaf tree. Those manifests become invalid.\\n- `restored-indexes` threads the field into `kv-storage` Settings so restored nodes “split at the published thresholds” ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:341)); `verify-candidate` and `restore` do the same ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1310), [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1344)). An emergent 3 or 7 would make all later mutation split at 3 or 7.\\n- The verified-frontier gate compares the manifest field to `:publish-opts` for equality before reusing a suffix frontier ([yin/repl/index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:368)). An emergent value varies per publication, so the gate would fail almost every round and force full reconstruction — or, if “fixed” by deleting the comparison, would reuse frontiers without the check that validates them. Do not remove that comparison.\\n- One field cannot describe four trees. Byte cuts give EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET different leaf counts over the same datoms. There is no single emergent count to record.\\n- The manifest is a hash input (`jing/segment-key manifest`, [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:619)). Redefining the field churns every manifest address and defeats the cache-sharing the directive wants to preserve, for scalar trees that never had the problem.\\n\\nRequired (R3): keep the dual constraint and the schema. Cut a page when either the byte budget or the count cap (512) is reached. `:branching-factor` keeps meaning “the count cap this tree was built and must be restored with”; the manifest stays exactly `{:indexes :count :branching-factor}`. If the budget must later be recorded for restore fidelity, that is a schema proposal to the work log per the directive’s own rule — not this slice, and not a reason to overload the existing field.\\n\\n## 5. Reverting `cb2cbe11` (`:repair-slots 48`) — REJECTED as a step of this change\\n\\nThe brief’s hedge (“verify rather than assume”) is right; its presumption is wrong, and its premise — “the 8× amplification collapses” — does not hold. At a 48 KiB budget, code-heavy REPL pages hold roughly the same datom counts as fix 5’s 64-key pages, so per-round address counts stay in fix 5’s 16–34 range, not the 512-era 9–16. The directive itself already says this correctly: page count per round still scales with volume, so the cadence question remains open.\\n\\nThe live evidence also already superseded the brief: `/tmp/nrf-branch-wt` carries an uncommitted sweep replacing 48 with a measured 40 (49 idle ticks in a 400-tick observation, against 32 at 48 and 3 at 32; queue peaks 221 at 16 slots, 133 at 24, 81 at 32). A blanket `git revert cb2cbe11` returns the store to the `dao.space.dht` default of 16 — the configuration measured never to drain.\\n\\nRequired (R4): no revert in the byte-cut change. After byte-cut lands, re-run the dart ticker-idle test at 16, 40, and 48 with byte-cut pages and pin the measured winner. Retire fix 5’s `:branching-factor 64` override in the same change (R3 makes it redundant); keep its commit in history, as the brief says.\\n\\n## 6. Sequencing — implement now on `nightly-reds-fix` (owner redirect upheld)\\n\\nThe directive’s “after master-green, do not fold into nightly-reds-fix” was written before the owner halted the slot sweep as solving the wrong problem. Merging the branch as-is would land a known-wrong workaround (64-key pages plus slot tuning fitted to them) and then immediately rework it. The branch is also the only place the failing configuration reproduces. Implement byte-cut there, with independent review before merge.\\n\\nRisks owned with this pick: the change touches bulk-build behavior on a nightly-fix branch, so the merge waits on the brief’s full dart + node matrix plus the index namespaces; pin churn (see Missed) must be reported as measured emergent counts, never fitted. Worktree hygiene needs care first: `/tmp/nrf-branch-wt` is detached at `4f772d61` with the uncommitted 40-slot edit, while `nightly-reds-fix` names `cb2cbe11`. Reconcile that state explicitly before any implementation commit; do not let the slot edit ride along silently.\\n\\nThe alternative’s risk is worse: master-green merges fix 5, the repair cadence gets tuned to the wrong page shape a second time, and the byte work starts over on a new slice.\\n\\n## 7. The 48 KiB budget — DEMAND MEASUREMENT; number not confirmed\\n\\n48 KiB is safe but unjustified, and as specified it is wired wrong:\\n\\n- It hardcodes 49,152 in `dao.space.index`, while the bound it derives from is per-composition data (`::max-message-bytes` in [dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:73), overridable at composition). Raise the composition bound and the index still over-cuts; lower it and the index overflows. Required (R5): derive the budget at the publish boundary — budget = composition `max-message-bytes` − measured `:store` envelope − margin — and pass it through `:publish-opts`. The envelope oracle already exists: `store-message-size` with maximal `q` and full cookie ([dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:228)). Tests must assert against the full store message, not the leaf alone.\\n- The headroom is far larger than the envelope needs. The only measurement on record shows 223 bytes of envelope (26,497 → 26,720). Reserving 16 KiB inflates page count by roughly a third versus a ~60 KiB budget, which directly worsens the repair economics in §5. But S3 measured small datoms only; no REPL code-datom leaf distribution has been measured at any budget.\\n- Required (R6): before pinning a number, measure real REPL covered-index leaves (the dart `yin.repl.dht-test` tree) — leaf byte distribution, emergent counts per index, per-round address counts. Then set the budget from the measured envelope plus an stated margin. 49,152 may stand as a provisional conservative pin only if the brief says that is what it is.\\n\\n## What both artifacts missed entirely\\n\\n1. Branch sizing is undercounted in the brief. A branch blob is `{:level n :keys [separator datoms] :addresses [...]}` ([btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:1080)). “Each entry’s bytes = its canonical address encoding” omits the separator keys — full datoms, the largest part — plus map/array framing. Branch cuts must account the whole blob with the R1 summation method, or branches overflow after leaves are fixed.\\n2. Bulk fill invariants. `split-ranges` builds chunks between `min-bf` and `max` aiming at the average ([btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:1998)), and branch levels group child nodes by the same count ranges. Byte grouping replaces this wholesale. The design must state that byte-cut leaves may be underfull by count, that this is legal for published immutable trees, and how a restored underfull leaf then behaves under `conj`/`disj` borrow/merge at `min-bf = bf/2`.\\n3. Oversize publication semantics. `publish-index!` appends blobs to the intake before the DHT ever refuses them; an over-budget leaf therefore yields a locally durable, remotely unacknowledged manifest — the original symptom in quieter form. The design must surface that as a publication failure at publish time (R2’s emission check does), not leave it to ack collapse.\\n4. Budget threading for the incremental session. The REPL indexer’s `:publish-opts` reach only the full-reconstruction path; the suffix path’s session is rebuilt by `index/restore` from the manifest. Unless the budget is threaded through `restore`/`session` and the storage Settings’ composition, the two paths will silently use different budgets after a restart.\\n5. Test pins beyond the four shape pins. Grep shows the load-bearing ones: the default-factor pin `(= 512 (:branching-factor manifest))` and the many `{:branching-factor 4}` fixtures in [index_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/index_test.cljc:163), the manifest-shape assertions at [index_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/index_test.cljc:573), and the DHT test’s `(= 64 … :branching-factor)` pin added by fix 5. Under R3 the 512/4 semantics survive; only the fix-5 pin and any realized-count assertions change, each reported with its measured value per measure-then-pin.\\n6. The manifest blob itself and non-DHT intake. Pages also travel intake streams and local stores that have no 64 KiB bound. Byte cutting is harmless there, but the design should say the budget is a DHT-derived composition input (R5), not a new property of the index format — otherwise a future non-DHT composition inherits an unexplained page shape.\\n\\n## Required changes for the re-brief\\n\\n- R1: encode-once length summation for leaf and branch cuts; exact header accounting; no candidate re-encodes (§2).\\n- R2: emission-bound check plus bulk-rebuild fallback on the incremental `publish!` path; budget threaded through `session`/`restore` (§3, Missed 4).\\n- R3: dual constraint (byte budget and 512 count cap); `:branching-factor` semantics and manifest schema unchanged; retire only the fix-5 `64` override (§4, §5).\\n- R4: no `cb2cbe11` revert in this change; slot count re-measured after landing (§5).\\n- R5: budget derived from the composition’s `max-message-bytes` minus the `store-message-size` envelope, passed via `:publish-opts`; tests assert full store-message size (§7).\\n- R6: REPL leaf measurement precedes pinning the budget number; 49,152 provisional only (§7).\\n- R7: sequence on `nightly-reds-fix` now, after reconciling the detached worktree and its uncommitted 40-slot edit; full dart + node matrix before merge (§6).\\n\\nWith R1–R7 folded in, this is approved to implement.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:11:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md collab/dao-stream/1794057236000-orchestrator-owner-addendum-bound-is-stream-wide.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\n\\n## Core Philosophy\\n\\nEverything is data.\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\n\\n### Foundational Axioms\\n\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\n   At system scale, a \\\"system\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\n\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\n\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\n\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\n\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\n\\n## Streams\\n\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\n\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\nthe stream's own.\\n\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\n\\n### Host Boundaries\\n\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\n\\n## Architectural Commitments\\n\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\n\\n## Design Principles\\n\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\n\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\n\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\n  ordinary streams.\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\n  auto-index observer.\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\n\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\nretired `:global` tag in\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\n\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\n\\n## Code Style\\n\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\n\\n## Development Workflow\\n\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\n  leaves to review.\\n\\n### Problem Decomposition\\n\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\n2. Identify invariants.\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\n\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\n\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-07 12:24:18 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-07 19:24:18 +07\\nCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode / glm-5.3-flash, master-green orchestrator seat)\\nSession-ID: not-applicable (interactive seat)\\n\\n# OWNER ADDENDUM: the message-bound problem is GENERAL to every dao.stream implementation\\n\\n## The owner's words (2026-11-07, during the byte-cut design review, verbatim)\\n\\n> \\\"but this problem is a general problem that all dao.stream implementation faces\\\"\\n\\n## What this changes\\n\\nThe first architect verdict (collab/dao-space/1794057586000-architect-byte-cut-\\ndesign-review.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log, APPROVED-WITH-CHANGES R1-R7) scoped\\nbyte-sized cutting to the INDEX BUILDER (dao.space.index) as the consumer that\\nhappens to carry large datoms. The owner's generalization relocates the design\\nquestion: EVERY dao.stream implementation composes messages that must respect\\nthe transport bound (dao.jing.dht ::max-message-bytes 65536, refused\\nfail-closed at send), and each one currently re-derives that discipline\\nprivately (or discovers the bound by refusal, as the nightly did).\\n\\nRequired consequence for the reconciliation round (next architect step, after\\nthe gpt-6-astra audit lands): adjudicate the LOCUS of the fix —\\n\\n(a) index-builder-local (what R1-R7 currently specify): only the index store\\n    cuts by bytes; every other dao.stream consumer keeps its own discipline;\\n(b) dao.stream-layer: the bound becomes a first-class stream/transport\\n    contract — e.g. the composition exposes its message budget as a queryable\\n    property, and/or dao.stream provides standardized segmentation (a\\n    chunked-message-sequence protocol) so oversized logical payloads are cut\\n    into bounded messages ONCE, at the stream layer, with reassembly\\n    semantics every peer already implements;\\n(c) some split: transport exposes the budget + refuses overbound (already\\n    true); stream layer offers optional segmentation; consumers like the\\n    index builder still self-cut because their payload has internal structure\\n    (sorted pages) that only they can split semantically.\\n\\nPoint (c) is the likely landing zone — the index page cut is semantic\\n(the datom order is the index; only the builder knows legal cut points),\\nwhile the bound knowledge should come FROM the composition, not be hardcoded\\n(R5 already requires deriving the budget from max-message-bytes; the\\ngeneralization says every consumer must derive it the same way). But that is\\nthe architects' call, not this seat's.\\n\\n## Status of in-flight work\\n\\ngpt-6-astra's audit of R1-R7 is in flight (read-only; dispatched before this\\naddendum — it will NOT have seen this file). Its verdict, plus this addendum,\\ngo to the reconciliation step before any engineer re-brief. No engineer\\ndispatch happens until the locus question is adjudicated.\\n\\n— the master-green orchestrator seat\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:12:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"wc -l src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*chunk*' src test; rg -n 'encode|header|identifier|write-map|write-array|canonical' src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:13:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"wc -l src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*chunk*' src test; rg -n 'encode|header|identifier|write-map|write-array|canonical' src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"    1414 src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc\\n    2163 src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc\\n    1283 src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc\\n    1363 src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc\\n     394 src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc\\n     571 src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc\\n    1139 src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc\\n    8327 total\\ntest/dao/stream/chunks_test.cljc\\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/chunks.cljc\\n2:  \\\"DaoJing's canonical CBOR value codec and portable numeric operations\\n5:   `encode` turns one supported value into its canonical payload bytes;\\n6:   `decode` accepts exactly one canonical payload and returns its value.\\n11:   Encoding normalizes a value and directly emits canonical CBOR bytes over\\n15:     over `[namespace name]`, read from the identifier's fields;\\n22:   * sets: tag 258 over elements sorted by their canonical bytes;\\n31:   decoded value to re-encode to the identical bytes.\\n34:   corpus refusal classes as a keyword (`:non-canonical`,\\n375:   kind, decimal scale and zero sign; canonical NaNs are equal. Symmetric\\n412:(defn- identifier-key\\n426:  \\\"True for the host byte array `encode` returns and `decode` accepts:\\n438:    (or (keyword? x) (symbol? x)) (hash (identifier-key x))\\n453:   identifiers by their namespace and name fields, lists equal to vectors\\n455:   metadata ignored. This is the equality whose collapses encode and\\n464:         (= (identifier-key a) (identifier-key b)))\\n518:   sign counts, and every NaN is the one canonical NaN), within decimal the\\n542:   their kind-strict identity, byte strings by content, identifiers by their\\n548:   identifiers). The same traversal as equiv, with the kind-strict numeric\\n554:    (or (keyword? x) (symbol? x)) [::identifier (identifier-key x)]\\n566:   width is not significant, and canonical NaNs are equal (every NaN has\\n567:   the one canonical encoding). Collections recurse the way equiv does,\\n582:;; Encode: direct canonical CBOR byte writer (RFC 8949) over dao.host.*\\n609:  \\\"The deepest CBOR item nesting encode emits and decode accepts. The\\n637:  \\\"Emits canonical shortest header bytes for major type `major` (0..7)\\n684:(defn- compare-canonical-bytes\\n686:   a proper prefix sorting first (RFC 8949 canonical order).\\\"\\n738:        tag-name (host text/utf8-encode \\\"dao.jing/float64\\\")\\n778:   refusing duplicate canonical encodings (dup-class) and portable-equality\\n799:        sorted (sort #(compare-canonical-bytes (first %1) (first %2)) wired)\\n812:        sorted (sort #(compare-canonical-bytes (first %1) (first %2)) wired)\\n819:  \\\"The bytes of x at depth d, where (build depth) encodes x's own\\n837:              (let [name-bytes (host text/utf8-encode \\\"clojure/with-meta\\\")\\n847:(defn- identifier-bytes\\n851:  (let [tag-name-bytes (host text/utf8-encode tag-name-str)\\n854:                   (let [ub (host text/utf8-encode (check-text ns-str))]\\n858:        name-ub (host text/utf8-encode name-str)\\n872:  (let [tag-name-bytes (host text/utf8-encode \\\"dao.jing/list\\\")\\n883:  \\\"The canonical CBOR bytes of one value whose outermost item sits at depth d,\\n890:                    (let [ub (host text/utf8-encode (check-text x))]\\n892:    (keyword? x) (identifier-bytes \\\"dao.jing/keyword\\\" x d)\\n893:    (symbol? x) (meta-bytes x d #(identifier-bytes \\\"dao.jing/symbol\\\" x %))\\n913:(defn encode\\n914:  \\\"The canonical CBOR payload bytes of one supported value (byte[] on the\\n929:(defn encoded-compare\\n930:  \\\"Order two supported values by their canonical encodings: the shorter\\n931:   encoding first, then unsigned bytewise (note: canonical map and set encoding\\n933:   Returns -1, 0 or 1. It encodes both values, so callers reserve it for the\\n936:  (let [ea (encode a)\\n937:        eb (encode b)\\n1128:   apart (host identifier equality on ClojureScript compares the joined\\n1158:              (refuse :malformed-frame \\\"identifier payload is not [ns name]\\\"))]\\n1248:          39 (refuse :identifier-tag-39)\\n1260:   `decode` accepted them, or bytes this codec encoded itself. Refuses\\n1262:   but does not re-encode the value to prove canonicality -- that check\\n1275:  \\\"The value of one canonical Jing payload (byte[], Uint8Array or\\n1277:   refuses and also any payload whose value does not re-encode to exactly\\n1281:    (when-not (bytes= bs (encode value))\\n1282:      (refuse :non-canonical))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:16:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/chunks.cljc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:18:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/chunks.cljc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(ns dao.stream\\n  \\\"DaoStream v2: passive substrate for IO in datom.world.\\n\\n   Seven operations:\\n     Five handle operations (protocols implemented per declared surface):\\n       descriptor (universal reachability and identity projection)\\n       cursor     (reader surface)\\n       next       (reader surface)\\n       append!    (writer surface)\\n       close!     (closable surface)\\n     Two transport entry functions (per-transport, host-composed):\\n       create!    (creates a new logical stream from a creation specification)\\n       attach!    (attaches to an existing stream via portable descriptor)\\n\\n   Result Convention:\\n     Every operation returns an open outcome map keyed by :dao.stream/outcome.\\n     All qualified keywords live under :dao.stream/…\\n     Each operation has an exhaustive, closed outcome set.\\\"\\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [next]))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; The Seven Operations and Standard Keywords\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(def operations\\n  \\\"The seven DaoStream v2 operations.\\\"\\n  #{:create! :attach! :descriptor :cursor :next :append! :close!})\\n\\n\\n(def surfaces\\n  \\\"The public operational surfaces a handle may declare.\\\"\\n  #{:reader :writer :closable})\\n\\n\\n;; Anchors\\n(def anchor-oldest\\n  \\\"Earliest retained position on the stream.\\\"\\n  :dao.stream/oldest)\\n\\n\\n(def anchor-newest\\n  \\\"Positioned after the newest appended value (observes next arrival).\\\"\\n  :dao.stream/newest)\\n\\n\\n(def standard-anchors\\n  \\\"Contract-defined anchor keywords.\\\"\\n  #{anchor-oldest anchor-newest})\\n\\n\\n;; Closed outcome sets per operation (contract exhaustive tables)\\n(def outcomes-create\\n  #{:dao.stream/ok\\n    :dao.stream/invalid-spec\\n    :dao.stream/not-found\\n    :dao.stream/transport-error})\\n\\n\\n(def outcomes-attach\\n  #{:dao.stream/ok\\n    :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor\\n    :dao.stream/not-found\\n    :dao.stream/transport-error})\\n\\n\\n(def outcomes-descriptor\\n  #{:dao.stream/ok})\\n\\n\\n(def outcomes-cursor\\n  #{:dao.stream/ok\\n    :dao.stream/invalid-anchor\\n    :dao.stream/closed\\n    :dao.stream/refused\\n    :dao.stream/transport-error})\\n\\n\\n(def outcomes-next\\n  #{:dao.stream/ok\\n    :dao.stream/blocked\\n    :dao.stream/end\\n    :dao.stream/gap\\n    :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\\n    :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\\n    :dao.stream/refused\\n    :dao.stream/transport-error})\\n\\n\\n(def outcomes-append\\n  #{:dao.stream/ok\\n    :dao.stream/full\\n    :dao.stream/invalid-value\\n    :dao.stream/closed\\n    :dao.stream/refused\\n    :dao.stream/transport-error})\\n\\n\\n(def outcomes-close\\n  #{:dao.stream/ok})\\n\\n\\n(def operation-outcomes\\n  \\\"Map of operation keyword to its closed outcome set.\\\"\\n  {:create! outcomes-create\\n   :attach! outcomes-attach\\n   :descriptor outcomes-descriptor\\n   :cursor outcomes-cursor\\n   :next outcomes-next\\n   :append! outcomes-append\\n   :close! outcomes-close})\\n\\n\\n(def outcome-required-keys\\n  \\\"Map from [operation outcome] to required keys in the result map.\\\"\\n  {[:create! :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/handle}\\n   [:attach! :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/handle}\\n   [:descriptor :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/descriptor :dao.stream/identity}\\n   [:cursor :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/cursor}\\n   [:next :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/value :dao.stream/cursor}\\n   [:next :dao.stream/gap] #{:dao.stream/cursor}})\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Canonical Operation Resolution Helper\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn canonical-op\\n  \\\"Normalizes op to canonical keyword (:create!, :attach!, :descriptor,\\n   :cursor, :next, :append!, :close!). Supports aliases without '!'.\\\"\\n  [op]\\n  (case op\\n    (:create :create!) :create!\\n    (:attach :attach!) :attach!\\n    (:descriptor) :descriptor\\n    (:cursor) :cursor\\n    (:next) :next\\n    (:append :append!) :append!\\n    (:close :close!) :close!\\n    nil))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Handle Protocols (Five Handle Operations)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defprotocol IDaoStreamDescriptor\\n  \\\"Universal reachability and identity projection. Belongs to no surface:\\n   implemented by every handle regardless of declared reader/writer/closable surfaces.\\\"\\n\\n  (descriptor\\n    [handle]\\n    \\\"Returns descriptor outcome map:\\n     {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\\n      :dao.stream/descriptor <portable-descriptor>\\n      :dao.stream/identity <logical-stream-identity>}\\\"))\\n\\n\\n(defprotocol IDaoStreamReader\\n  \\\"Reader surface: positioned, non-destructive observation via immutable cursors.\\\"\\n\\n  (cursor\\n    [handle anchor]\\n    \\\"Mint an immutable cursor from anchor (:dao.stream/oldest, :dao.stream/newest,\\n     or transport-specific anchor).\\n     Returns outcome map with :dao.stream/cursor on :dao.stream/ok.\\\")\\n\\n  (next\\n    [handle cursor]\\n    \\\"Read the value at cursor position. Total and non-blocking.\\n     Returns read outcome map.\\\"))\\n\\n\\n(defprotocol IDaoStreamWriter\\n  \\\"Writer surface: append values to the sequence this handle's writer surface is on.\\\"\\n\\n  (append!\\n    [handle val]\\n    \\\"Append val to the sequence. Total and non-blocking.\\n     Returns write outcome map.\\\"))\\n\\n\\n(defprotocol IDaoStreamClosable\\n  \\\"Closable surface: close what this handle is on (logical stream or attachment).\\n   Idempotent.\\\"\\n\\n  (close!\\n    [handle]\\n    \\\"Closes what this handle is on. Irrevocable.\\n     Returns outcome map {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}.\\\"))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Surface Inspection & Gating\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn reader?\\n  \\\"True if handle implements the reader surface (IDaoStreamReader).\\\"\\n  [handle]\\n  (satisfies? IDaoStreamReader handle))\\n\\n\\n(defn writer?\\n  \\\"True if handle implements the writer surface (IDaoStreamWriter).\\\"\\n  [handle]\\n  (satisfies? IDaoStreamWriter handle))\\n\\n\\n(defn closable?\\n  \\\"True if handle implements the closable surface (IDaoStreamClosable).\\\"\\n  [handle]\\n  (satisfies? IDaoStreamClosable handle))\\n\\n\\n(defn descriptor?\\n  \\\"True if handle implements IDaoStreamDescriptor.\\\"\\n  [handle]\\n  (satisfies? IDaoStreamDescriptor handle))\\n\\n\\n(defn declared-surfaces\\n  \\\"Returns set of public surfaces implemented by handle:\\n   subset of #{:reader :writer :closable}.\\\"\\n  [handle]\\n  (cond-> #{}\\n    (reader? handle) (conj :reader)\\n    (writer? handle) (conj :writer)\\n    (closable? handle) (conj :closable)))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Result Convention & Envelope Validation\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn qualified-keyword?*\\n  \\\"True if x is a qualified keyword. Portable across CLJ, CLJS, CLJD.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (boolean (and (keyword? x) (namespace x))))\\n\\n\\n(defn outcome-map?\\n  \\\"True if x is an open outcome map with a qualified keyword under :dao.stream/outcome.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (and (map? x)\\n       (qualified-keyword?* (:dao.stream/outcome x))))\\n\\n\\n(defn validate-outcome\\n  \\\"Validates an operation outcome against contract requirements.\\n   Returns nil if valid, or a failure map describing the defect if invalid.\\\"\\n  [op result]\\n  (if-let [canon-op (canonical-op op)]\\n    (cond\\n      (not (outcome-map? result))\\n      {:valid? false :error :invalid-outcome-map :operation canon-op :result result}\\n\\n      :else\\n      (let [outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)\\n            allowed (get operation-outcomes canon-op)]\\n        (cond\\n          (not (contains? allowed outcome))\\n          {:valid? false\\n           :error :unauthorized-outcome\\n           :operation canon-op\\n           :outcome outcome\\n           :allowed allowed}\\n\\n          :else\\n          (let [req-keys (get outcome-required-keys [canon-op outcome] #{})\\n                missing (into #{} (remove #(contains? result %) req-keys))]\\n            (if (seq missing)\\n              {:valid? false\\n               :error :missing-required-keys\\n               :operation canon-op\\n               :outcome outcome\\n               :missing missing}\\n              nil)))))\\n    {:valid? false :error :unknown-operation :operation op}))\\n\\n\\n(defn valid-outcome?\\n  \\\"True if result is a valid outcome map for op according to the contract.\\\"\\n  [op result]\\n  (nil? (validate-outcome op result)))\\n\\n\\n(defn valid-envelope?\\n  \\\"Generic envelope validation: envelope must be a map with a qualified keyword\\n   under :dao.stream/type. Everything else is transport-owned.\\\"\\n  [envelope]\\n  (and (map? envelope)\\n       (qualified-keyword?* (:dao.stream/type envelope))))\\n\\n\\n(defn valid-creation-spec?\\n  \\\"True if spec is a generically valid creation specification envelope:\\n   must be a map with a qualified keyword under :dao.stream/type.\\\"\\n  [spec]\\n  (valid-envelope? spec))\\n\\n\\n(defn valid-descriptor?\\n  \\\"True if descriptor is a generically valid portable descriptor envelope:\\n   must be a valid envelope (:dao.stream/type present and qualified)\\n   and carry :dao.stream/identity.\\\"\\n  [descriptor]\\n  (and (valid-envelope? descriptor)\\n       (contains? descriptor :dao.stream/identity)))\\n\\n\\n(defn descriptor-identity-consistent?\\n  \\\"True if a descriptor operation result is {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok ...}\\n   where both :dao.stream/descriptor and :dao.stream/identity are present,\\n   descriptor is a valid descriptor envelope, and the sibling :dao.stream/identity\\n   projection structurally equals the identity inside the descriptor envelope.\\\"\\n  [result]\\n  (and (map? result)\\n       (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome result))\\n       (contains? result :dao.stream/descriptor)\\n       (contains? result :dao.stream/identity)\\n       (let [sibling-id (:dao.stream/identity result)\\n             desc (:dao.stream/descriptor result)]\\n         (and (some? sibling-id)\\n              (valid-descriptor? desc)\\n              (= sibling-id (:dao.stream/identity desc))))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Open Result Map Constructors\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn ok-result\\n  \\\"Convenience constructor for an open :dao.stream/ok outcome map.\\\"\\n  ([]\\n   {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok})\\n  ([extra-map]\\n   (assoc extra-map :dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok)))\\n\\n\\n(defn outcome-result\\n  \\\"Convenience constructor for an open outcome map.\\\"\\n  ([outcome]\\n   {:dao.stream/outcome outcome})\\n  ([outcome extra-map]\\n   (assoc extra-map :dao.stream/outcome outcome)))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Transport Entry Functions & Host Dispatch\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;;\\n;; Two transport entry functions: create! and attach!\\n;; These take no handle because none exists yet. These are per-transport functions,\\n;; host-composed, taking whatever composition state the transport needs.\\n;;\\n;; Putting create! or attach! on a protocol is how the retired registry grows back;\\n;; DaoStream v2 ships no dispatch machinery (no multimethod, no registry,\\n;; no load-time side effect).\\n;;\\n;; Dynamic dispatch on :dao.stream/type is performed by the host using an ordinary\\n;; host-owned lookup table:\\n;;\\n;;   {:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!\\n;;                            :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}\\n;;    :dao.stream/ws         {:dao.stream/attach ws/attach!}}\\n;;\\n;; The helper functions below provide transparent, purely functional host dispatch.\\n\\n(defn host-dispatch-create!\\n  \\\"Host dispatch helper: looks up :dao.stream/create in dispatch-table\\n   for (:dao.stream/type creation-spec).\\n   Returns :dao.stream/invalid-spec if spec is not a valid envelope.\\n   Returns :dao.stream/not-found if transport or :dao.stream/create is absent.\\n   Otherwise invokes the transport's create function.\\\"\\n  [dispatch-table creation-spec]\\n  (if-not (valid-creation-spec? creation-spec)\\n    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-spec}\\n    (if-let [create-fn (get-in dispatch-table [(:dao.stream/type creation-spec) :dao.stream/create])]\\n      (create-fn creation-spec)\\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/not-found})))\\n\\n\\n(defn host-dispatch-attach!\\n  \\\"Host dispatch helper: looks up :dao.stream/attach in dispatch-table\\n   for (:dao.stream/type descriptor).\\n   Returns :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor if descriptor is not a valid descriptor envelope.\\n   Returns :dao.stream/not-found if transport or :dao.stream/attach is absent.\\n   Otherwise invokes the transport's attach function.\\\"\\n  [dispatch-table descriptor]\\n  (if-not (valid-descriptor? descriptor)\\n    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor}\\n    (if-let [attach-fn (get-in dispatch-table [(:dao.stream/type descriptor) :dao.stream/attach])]\\n      (attach-fn descriptor)\\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/not-found})))\\n(ns dao.stream.chunks\\n  \\\"Transport-neutral splitting and bounded reassembly of host byte payloads,\\n   over the profile's `dao.host.bytes` (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md\\n   §4.2). `concat-bytes` writes only the fresh array it allocates, before\\n   returning it (§2.4).\\\"\\n  (:require\\n    [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]))\\n\\n\\n(defn length\\n  [bs]\\n  (bytes/length bs))\\n\\n\\n(defn empty-bytes\\n  [n]\\n  (bytes/alloc n))\\n\\n\\n(defn slice\\n  [bs start end]\\n  (bytes/slice bs start end))\\n\\n\\n(defn concat-bytes\\n  [pieces]\\n  (let [out (bytes/alloc (reduce + (map bytes/length pieces)))]\\n    (reduce (fn [offset bs]\\n              (let [n (bytes/length bs)]\\n                (bytes/copy! bs 0 out offset n)\\n                (+ offset n)))\\n            0 pieces)\\n    out))\\n\\n\\n(defn split\\n  \\\"Return protocol-specific envelopes fitting budget. envelope receives part,\\n   parts, bytes; encode measures the actual wire size.\\\"\\n  [payload budget envelope encode]\\n  (let [total (length payload)]\\n    (loop [size (min total budget)]\\n      (when-not (pos? size)\\n        (throw (ex-info \\\"chunk envelope cannot fit datagram\\\" {:budget budget})))\\n      (let [parts (max 1 (quot (+ total (dec size)) size))]\\n        (if (<= (length (encode (envelope (dec parts) parts (empty-bytes size))))\\n                budget)\\n          (mapv (fn [part]\\n                  (let [start (* part size)]\\n                    (envelope part parts\\n                              (slice payload start (min total (+ start size))))))\\n                (range parts))\\n          (recur (dec size)))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn absorb\\n  \\\"Return [state completed-bytes-or-nil]. Malformed, duplicate, inconsistent,\\n   and overbound pieces cannot increase held bytes. Evict oldest partial at\\n   the global count bound. Caller supplies a source-qualified key.\\\"\\n  [state key part parts piece max-bytes max-partials]\\n  (if-not (and (integer? part) (integer? parts) (pos? parts)\\n               (<= parts max-bytes) (<= 0 part) (< part parts)\\n               (bytes/bytes? piece) (pos? (length piece))\\n               (<= (length piece) max-bytes) (pos? max-partials))\\n    [state nil]\\n    (let [held (get-in state [:partial key])]\\n      (if (or (and held (not= parts (:parts-count held)))\\n              (and held (contains? (:parts held) part)))\\n        [state nil]\\n        (let [state (if held state\\n                        (-> state\\n                            (assoc-in [:partial key] {:parts-count parts :parts {} :bytes-total 0})\\n                            (update :partial-order (fnil conj []) key)))\\n              oldest (first (:partial-order state))\\n              state (if (> (count (:partial state)) max-partials)\\n                      (-> state (update :partial dissoc oldest)\\n                          (update :partial-order #(vec (rest %)))) state)\\n              held (get-in state [:partial key])]\\n          (if-not held [state nil]\\n                  (let [total (+ (:bytes-total held) (length piece))]\\n                    (if (> total max-bytes)\\n                      [(-> state (update :partial dissoc key)\\n                           (update :partial-order #(vec (remove #{key} %)))) nil]\\n                      (let [state (-> state (assoc-in [:partial key :parts part] piece)\\n                                      (assoc-in [:partial key :bytes-total] total))\\n                            held (get-in state [:partial key])]\\n                        (if (= parts (count (:parts held)))\\n                          [(-> state (update :partial dissoc key)\\n                               (update :partial-order #(vec (remove #{key} %))))\\n                           (concat-bytes (mapv (:parts held) (range parts)))]\\n                          [state nil]))))))))))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:24:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'min-bf|bf\\\\\\\\*|node-conj|node-disj|restore-tree|store-tree|node->blob|split-ranges|from-sorted|from-sequential|borrow|merge' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc; cat src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc; sed -n '1960,2163p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"11:  Node layout and split/merge/borrow invariants transcribe\\n26:  \\\"Returned by node-conj/node-disj when the key was already present/absent.\\\"\\n75:(defn- bf*\\n80:(defn- min-bf*\\n143:  (if (editing? sett) (min (bf* sett) (+ n expand-len)) n))\\n426:  (node-conj\\n430:  (node-disj\\n502:  (node-conj\\n520:            (< len (bf* settings)) (let [nl (inc len)\\n550:  (node-disj\\n559:            ;; nothing to merge\\n560:            (or (>= new-len (min-bf* settings))\\n573:            (and (some? left) (<= (+ llen new-len) (bf* settings)))\\n582:            (and (some? right) (<= (+ new-len rlen) (bf* settings)))\\n590:            ;; borrow from left\\n602:            ;; borrow from right\\n673:  (node-conj\\n680:              nodes (node-conj (node-child this ins) cmp k sett)]\\n715:            (< len (bf* settings))\\n804:  (node-disj\\n814:              nodes (node-disj (node-child this idx)\\n875:                (or (>= new-len (min-bf* settings))\\n898:                     (<= (+ (node-len left) new-len) (bf* settings)))\\n921:                     (<= (+ new-len (node-len right)) (bf* settings)))\\n942:                ;; borrow from left\\n977:                ;; borrow from right\\n1080:(defn node->blob\\n1188:   ref-type reference refaulted from address; store-tree is a no-op when\\n1193:  (-store-tree! [s storage])\\n1244:  (-store-tree!\\n1593:    (let [nodes (node-conj root cmp k settings)]\\n1622:    (let [nodes (node-disj root cmp k true nil nil settings)]\\n1644:    ;; until the first edit), so persistent!-then-store-tree stays a\\n1755:         nodes (node-conj (resident-root s) cmp k sett)]\\n1791:         nodes (node-disj (resident-root s) cmp k true nil nil sett)]\\n1995:;; Bulk build (from-sorted-array / from-sequential;\\n1998:(defn- split-ranges\\n2031:(defn from-sorted-array\\n2033:  ([cmp arr] (from-sorted-array cmp arr (arr/alength arr) nil))\\n2036:         mx (bf* sett)\\n2037:         avg (quot (+ (min-bf* sett) mx) 2)\\n2040:                      (split-ranges len avg mx))]\\n2056:                      (split-ranges (clojure.core/count nodes) avg mx))))))))\\n2059:(defn from-sequential\\n2061:  ([cmp coll] (from-sequential cmp coll nil))\\n2067:     (from-sorted-array cmp arr (distinct-sorted! arr cmp) opts))))\\n2082:(defn store-tree\\n2088:  (when (pos? (.-cnt s)) (-store-tree! s storage)))\\n2091:(defn restore-tree\\n(ns dao.data.btree.storage\\n  \\\"IStorage adapters over dao.jing content-store handles (docs/design/dao.data.btree.md\\n   sections 5.1, 5.4). A handle is the plain-data byte store carrying\\n   :put-bytes-fn, :get-bytes-fn, and :close-fn (docs/design/dao.jing.md,\\n   Materialization rule); addresses are derived from payloads by\\n   dao.jing/materialize!, never supplied by this layer. Two adapters, one\\n   error taxonomy:\\n\\n   - KVStorage (kv-storage): the sync rule-1 adapter. Absence is\\n     authoritative: a missing blob throws \\\\\\\"missing index segment\\\\\\\". Every\\n     read goes through `dao.jing/get`, which ALWAYS hash-verifies the stored\\n     bytes against the content address and decodes them. The optional\\n     `:verify?` flag (§5.2) is a different, additional check: it re-serializes\\n     the decoded blob and compares its key to the address, mismatch throwing\\n     \\\\\\\"corrupt index segment\\\\\\\". That re-serialization check is off by\\n     default because dao.jing's print-based content hash is not host-stable,\\n     so cross-host reads must not use it (dao.jing.md, Current Scope).\\n   - `read-node-blob`: ONE verified read of ONE node blob for a caller that\\n     drives traversal itself (the checked cursor,\\n     dao.data.btree.cursor); it restores nothing and builds no node.\\n   - HydrationStorage (hydration-storage): the §5.4 hydration-cache\\n     adapter for async-only backends. Reads answer only from the cache;\\n     any miss throws \\\\\\\"unhydrated segment\\\\\\\" — the cache cannot distinguish\\n     absent from not-yet-fetched without blocking on the backend. hydrate!\\n     copies the reachable blob graph from the source into the cache. The\\n     source is either a sync content handle or an async one\\n     (dao.jing.content.async/async-content); over an async source the\\n     non-blocking hydrate-async and store-tree-async are the only way in\\n     and out, and the sync store path throws.\\n\\n   dao.data.btree itself stays storage-agnostic; this namespace is the one\\n   place the tree meets dao.jing (Decision 1: no new storage protocol —\\n   everything below is materialize!/get, plus segment-matches? for §5.2\\n   verification).\\\"\\n  (:require #?@(:cljd [[\\\"dart:async\\\" :as async]])\\n            [dao.data.btree :as bt]\\n            [dao.jing :as jing]))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private absent\\n  \\\"Identity sentinel for handle misses (§5.1 sketch): keywords are not\\n   reliably `identical?` on cljs, an opaque host object is.\\\"\\n  #?(:cljd (Object.)\\n     :clj (Object.)\\n     :cljs (js-obj)))\\n\\n\\n(defn read-node-blob\\n  \\\"Read the decoded blob of exactly ONE node: one `dao.jing/get` (which\\n   hash-verifies the stored bytes against `address` and decodes them), and\\n   nothing else. It does not build a node, traverse or hydrate children, open\\n   a handle, cache, or retry. An absent address throws \\\\\\\"missing index\\n   segment\\\\\\\" with {:address address}; any failure `jing/get` raises (for\\n   example corrupt bytes) propagates unchanged so its cause is preserved.\\\"\\n  [store address]\\n  (let [blob (jing/get store address absent)]\\n    (when (identical? blob absent)\\n      (throw (ex-info \\\"missing index segment\\\" {:address address})))\\n    blob))\\n\\n\\n(deftype KVStorage\\n  [store settings verify? algorithm]\\n\\n  bt/IStorage\\n\\n  (-store\\n    [_ node]\\n    (let [blob (bt/node->blob node)]\\n      (if algorithm\\n        (jing/materialize! store blob {:algorithm algorithm})\\n        (jing/materialize! store blob))))\\n\\n\\n  (-restore\\n    [_ addr]\\n    (let [blob (jing/get store addr absent)]\\n      (when (identical? blob absent)\\n        (throw (ex-info \\\"missing index segment\\\" {:address addr})))\\n      (when verify?\\n        (when-not (jing/segment-matches? addr blob)\\n          (throw (ex-info \\\"corrupt index segment\\\"\\n                          {:expected addr, :actual (jing/segment-key blob)}))))\\n      (bt/blob->node blob settings)))\\n\\n\\n  (-accessed [_ _addr] nil)\\n\\n\\n  (-settings [_] settings))\\n\\n\\n(defn kv-storage\\n  \\\"A sync IStorage over a dao.jing content-store handle. opts:\\n   {:branching-factor n (default 512) :ref-type k (default per host, §5.3)\\n   :verify? bool (default false, §5.2 — same-host mint+read only)\\n   :algorithm keyword (default nil, mints with default-hash-algorithm)}. The\\n   returned storage owns the Settings every tree restored through it shares\\n   (§5.1 threading rule); pass the manifest's :branching-factor here.\\\"\\n  ([store] (kv-storage store nil))\\n  ([store opts]\\n   (let [box (volatile! nil)\\n         sett (bt/->Settings (or (:branching-factor opts) 512)\\n                             (or (:ref-type opts) (bt/default-ref-type*))\\n                             nil\\n                             box)\\n         storage (KVStorage. store\\n                             sett\\n                             (boolean (:verify? opts))\\n                             (:algorithm opts))]\\n     (vreset! box storage)\\n     storage)))\\n\\n\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n;; Hydration cache (§5.4)\\n\\n(defn- async-source?\\n  \\\"True for an async content handle (dao.jing.content.async/async-content).\\\"\\n  [source]\\n  (some? (:get-content-async-fn source)))\\n\\n\\n(deftype HydrationStorage\\n  [source cache settings outbox algorithm]\\n  ;; outbox: atom {:writing? bool, :unacked [[addr blob] ...]} — used\\n  ;; only over an async source. :writing? is true only inside\\n  ;; store-tree-async; :unacked holds every blob stored to the cache whose\\n  ;; source write has not yet acknowledged, carried across failed calls so\\n  ;; a retry re-pushes them (§5.4 durability ordering).\\n\\n  bt/IStorage\\n\\n  (-store\\n    [_ node]\\n    (let [blob (bt/node->blob node)\\n          opts (when algorithm {:algorithm algorithm})]\\n      (if (async-source? source)\\n        (do\\n          ;; the sync store-tree cannot wait for acknowledgments (§5.4)\\n          (when-not (:writing? @outbox)\\n            (throw (ex-info \\\"sync store-tree against an async backend\\\" {})))\\n          (let [addr (if opts\\n                       (jing/materialize! cache blob opts)\\n                       (jing/materialize! cache blob))]\\n            (swap! outbox update :unacked\\n                   (fn [u]\\n                     (if (some #(= addr (first %)) u) u (conj u [addr blob]))))\\n            addr))\\n        ;; writes land in the durable source AND the read cache; both are\\n        ;; content-addressed, so both must answer with the same address\\n        (let [source-addr (if opts\\n                            (jing/materialize! source blob opts)\\n                            (jing/materialize! source blob))\\n              cache-addr (if opts\\n                           (jing/materialize! cache blob opts)\\n                           (jing/materialize! cache blob))]\\n          (when-not (= source-addr cache-addr)\\n            (throw (ex-info \\\"hydration source and cache diverged\\\"\\n                            {:source source-addr, :cache cache-addr})))\\n          source-addr))))\\n\\n\\n  (-restore\\n    [_ addr]\\n    (let [blob (jing/get cache addr absent)]\\n      (if (identical? blob absent)\\n        ;; absent vs not-hydrated is undecidable synchronously (§5.4)\\n        (throw (ex-info \\\"unhydrated segment\\\" {:address addr}))\\n        (bt/blob->node blob settings))))\\n\\n\\n  (-accessed [_ _addr] nil)\\n\\n\\n  (-settings [_] settings))\\n\\n\\n(defn hydration-storage\\n  \\\"The §5.4 hydration-cache adapter: reads answer only from `cache` (miss\\n   => \\\\\\\"unhydrated segment\\\\\\\"); `hydrate!` fills the cache from `source`.\\n   `source` is a sync content handle (hydrate!, store-tree) or an async\\n   one from dao.jing.content.async (hydrate-async, store-tree-async).\\\"\\n  ([source cache] (hydration-storage source cache nil))\\n  ([source cache opts]\\n   (let [box (volatile! nil)\\n         sett (bt/->Settings (or (:branching-factor opts) 512)\\n                             (or (:ref-type opts) (bt/default-ref-type*))\\n                             nil\\n                             box)\\n         storage (HydrationStorage. source\\n                                    cache\\n                                    sett\\n                                    (atom {:writing? false, :unacked []})\\n                                    (:algorithm opts))]\\n     (vreset! box storage)\\n     storage)))\\n\\n\\n(defn hydrate!\\n  \\\"Full-graph hydration (§5.4 rule 2, blocking variant): copy every blob\\n   reachable from the set's root address out of the source backend into\\n   the hydration cache, so subsequent reads — and mutations, whose write\\n   path requires residency — succeed. Idempotent: already-cached segments\\n   are re-materialized as no-ops. Returns the set. On a non-hydration\\n   storage this is a no-op (sync backends need no hydration).\\\"\\n  [s]\\n  (let [storage (bt/set-storage s)]\\n    (when (instance? HydrationStorage storage)\\n      (let [^HydrationStorage hs storage\\n            source (.-source hs)\\n            cache (.-cache hs)]\\n        (when (async-source? source)\\n          (throw (ex-info \\\"hydrate! against an async backend; use hydrate-async\\\"\\n                          {})))\\n        (letfn [(pull!\\n                  [addr]\\n                  (let [blob (jing/get source addr absent)]\\n                    (when (identical? blob absent)\\n                      (throw (ex-info \\\"missing index segment\\\" {:address addr})))\\n                    (let [algo (jing/segment-algorithm addr)\\n                          addr' (jing/materialize! cache blob\\n                                                   {:algorithm algo})]\\n                      (when-not (= addr addr')\\n                        (throw (ex-info \\\"hydration address mismatch\\\"\\n                                        {:expected addr, :actual addr'}))))\\n                    (doseq [a (:addresses blob)] (pull! a))))]\\n          (when-some [addr (bt/set-address s)] (pull! addr)))))\\n    s))\\n\\n\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n;; Async variants (§5.4 rule 2 non-blocking, and durability ordering)\\n\\n(defn- deferred\\n  \\\"The host's one-shot async value: a Promise (cljs), a Completer-backed\\n   Future (cljd), a CompletableFuture (JVM). `start` receives resolve and\\n   reject fns and calls exactly one of them.\\\"\\n  [start]\\n  #?(:cljd (let [c (async/Completer)]\\n             (start #(.complete c %) #(.completeError c %))\\n             (.-future c))\\n     :clj (let [f (java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture.)]\\n            (start #(.complete f %) #(.completeExceptionally f %))\\n            f)\\n     :cljs (js/Promise. (fn [resolve reject] (start resolve reject)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- attempt\\n  \\\"Run thunk `f`, answering [:ok v] or [:err e] — so a callback is never\\n   called from inside the try that guards the work it reports on.\\\"\\n  [f]\\n  (try [:ok (f)]\\n       (catch #?(:cljd Object\\n                 :clj Throwable\\n                 :cljs :default)\\n              e\\n         [:err e])))\\n\\n\\n(defn- report\\n  [[tag x] on-ok on-err]\\n  (if (= :ok tag) (on-ok x) (on-err x)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- async-hydration-storage\\n  \\\"The storage when it is a HydrationStorage over an async source, else nil.\\\"\\n  [storage]\\n  (when (and (instance? HydrationStorage storage)\\n             (async-source? (.-source ^HydrationStorage storage)))\\n    storage))\\n\\n\\n(defn hydrate-async\\n  \\\"Full-graph hydration (§5.4 rule 2, non-blocking variant): fetch every\\n   blob reachable from the set's root address out of the async source into\\n   the hydration cache, then resolve to the same set. Addresses already in\\n   the cache are read from it rather than fetched, so hydrating a resident\\n   graph issues no requests. Over a sync source it runs hydrate!; over a\\n   non-hydration storage it resolves at once.\\n\\n   (hydrate-async s)              => Promise / Future / CompletableFuture\\n   (hydrate-async s on-ok on-err) => nil; exactly one callback is called\\n\\n   A source miss rejects \\\\\\\"missing index segment\\\\\\\" (the source answers\\n   absence authoritatively); an `:error` or `:lost` completion rejects\\n   \\\\\\\"hydration fetch failed\\\\\\\" with the completion attached.\\\"\\n  ([s] (deferred (fn [resolve reject] (hydrate-async s resolve reject))))\\n  ([s on-ok on-err]\\n   (if-let [^HydrationStorage hs (async-hydration-storage (bt/set-storage s))]\\n     (let [cache (.-cache hs)\\n           get-async (:get-content-async-fn (.-source hs))\\n           settled (atom false)\\n           ok! #(when (compare-and-set! settled false true) (on-ok s))\\n           fail! #(when (compare-and-set! settled false true) (on-err %))\\n           ;; one token for the walk itself plus one per visited address\\n           outstanding (atom 1)\\n           seen (atom #{})\\n           done! #(when (zero? (swap! outstanding dec)) (ok!))]\\n       (letfn [(visit!\\n                 [addr]\\n                 (when-not (contains? (first (swap-vals! seen conj addr)) addr)\\n                   (swap! outstanding inc)\\n                   (let [blob (jing/get cache addr absent)]\\n                     (if (identical? blob absent)\\n                       (get-async addr #(report (attempt (fn [] (fetched! addr %)))\\n                                                (fn [_] nil)\\n                                                fail!))\\n                       (do (doseq [a (:addresses blob)] (visit! a))\\n                           (done!))))))\\n               (fetched!\\n                 [addr c]\\n                 (cond\\n                   (not (contains? c :found?))\\n                   (fail! (ex-info \\\"hydration fetch failed\\\"\\n                                   {:address addr, :completion c}))\\n\\n                   (not (:found? c))\\n                   (fail! (ex-info \\\"missing index segment\\\" {:address addr}))\\n\\n                   :else\\n                   (let [blob (:value c)\\n                         algo (jing/segment-algorithm addr)\\n                         addr' (jing/materialize! cache blob\\n                                                  {:algorithm algo})]\\n                     (if (= addr addr')\\n                       (do (doseq [a (:addresses blob)] (visit! a))\\n                           (done!))\\n                       (fail! (ex-info \\\"hydration address mismatch\\\"\\n                                       {:expected addr, :actual addr'}))))))]\\n         (report (attempt (fn []\\n                            (when-some [addr (bt/set-address s)] (visit! addr))\\n                            (done!)))\\n                 (fn [_] nil)\\n                 fail!))\\n       nil)\\n     (report (attempt #(hydrate! s)) on-ok on-err))))\\n\\n\\n(defn store-tree-async\\n  \\\"The write-side sibling of hydrate-async (§5.4 durability ordering):\\n   store the set's dirty subgraph into the hydration cache, push every\\n   unacknowledged segment to the async source, and resolve to the root\\n   address only after every one of those writes has acknowledged. The\\n   caller chains the root `cas!` on that resolution — never before.\\n\\n   A segment whose write fails stays queued on the storage and is pushed\\n   again by the next store-tree-async through it, so retrying a failed\\n   call — even on the now-addressed set, whose store-tree is a no-op —\\n   cannot resolve over an incomplete closure. Over a sync storage it\\n   resolves to store-tree's result. An empty set resolves to nil, as\\n   store-tree answers.\\n\\n   (store-tree-async s storage)              => Promise / Future / CompletableFuture\\n   (store-tree-async s storage on-ok on-err) => nil; exactly one callback is called\\\"\\n  ([s storage]\\n   (deferred (fn [resolve reject] (store-tree-async s storage resolve reject))))\\n  ([s storage on-ok on-err]\\n   (if-let [^HydrationStorage hs (async-hydration-storage storage)]\\n     (let [outbox (.-outbox hs)\\n           source (.-source hs)\\n           put-content-async (or (:put-content-async-fn source)\\n                                 (when-let [m (:materialize-async-fn source)]\\n                                   (fn [addr blob cb]\\n                                     (m blob\\n                                        {:algorithm\\n                                         (jing/segment-algorithm addr)}\\n                                        cb))))\\n           stored (attempt (fn []\\n                             (swap! outbox assoc :writing? true)\\n                             (try (bt/store-tree s storage)\\n                                  (finally (swap! outbox assoc :writing? false)))))]\\n       (if (= :err (first stored))\\n         (report stored on-ok on-err)\\n         (let [root (second stored)\\n               unacked (:unacked @outbox)\\n               remaining (atom (count unacked))\\n               failure (atom nil)\\n               settle! #(when (zero? (swap! remaining dec))\\n                          (if-some [e @failure] (on-err e) (on-ok root)))]\\n           (if (empty? unacked)\\n             (on-ok root)\\n             (doseq [[addr blob] unacked]\\n               (put-content-async\\n                 addr\\n                 blob\\n                 (fn [c]\\n                   (if (and (:result c) (= addr (:address c)))\\n                     ;; acknowledged: the one place a segment leaves the queue\\n                     (swap! outbox update :unacked\\n                            (fn [u] (filterv #(not= addr (first %)) u)))\\n                     (compare-and-set!\\n                       failure\\n                       nil\\n                       (ex-info \\\"store-tree-async: segment write failed\\\"\\n                                {:address addr, :completion c})))\\n                   (settle!)))))))\\n       nil)\\n     (report (attempt #(bt/store-tree s storage)) on-ok on-err))))\\n                         (when-let [st (descend-from-r root hi cmp)]\\n                           (if (and (not hi-incl?)\\n                                    (zero? (cmp (stack-key st) hi)))\\n                             (retreat st)\\n                             st)))]\\n        (non-empty (stack-rseq stack lo lo-incl? cmp))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn slice\\n  \\\"Lazy ascending seq of all X with from <= X <= to (nil bound = unbounded);\\n   nil when empty. Explicit-comparator arity per psset.\\\"\\n  ([^BTSet s from to] (slice s from to (.-cmp s)))\\n  ([^BTSet s from to cmp] (slice* s from true to true cmp)))\\n\\n\\n(defn rslice\\n  \\\"Lazy descending seq of all X with to <= X <= from, iterating from `from`\\n   downward (nil bound = unbounded); nil when empty.\\\"\\n  ([^BTSet s from to] (rslice s from to (.-cmp s)))\\n  ([^BTSet s from to cmp] (rslice* s from true to true cmp)))\\n\\n\\n(defn seq\\n  \\\"Lazy ascending seq of the whole set; nil when empty.\\\"\\n  [^BTSet s]\\n  (slice s nil nil))\\n\\n\\n(defn rseq\\n  \\\"Lazy descending seq of the whole set; nil when empty.\\\"\\n  [^BTSet s]\\n  (rslice s nil nil))\\n\\n\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n;; Bulk build (from-sorted-array / from-sequential;\\n;; persistent_sorted_set.clj:99-135 — the §5.2 fill policy)\\n\\n(defn- split-ranges\\n  \\\"[from to) chunk ranges over len items, chunks between min and max keys,\\n   aiming for avg — transcribes `split` (persistent_sorted_set.clj:63).\\\"\\n  [len avg mx]\\n  (loop [acc []\\n         from 0]\\n    (let [l (- len from)]\\n      (cond (== 0 l) acc\\n            (>= l (* 2 avg)) (recur (clojure.core/conj acc [from (+ from avg)])\\n                                    (+ from avg))\\n            (<= l mx) (clojure.core/conj acc [from len])\\n            :else (let [half (+ from (quot l 2))]\\n                    (-> acc\\n                        (clojure.core/conj [from half])\\n                        (clojure.core/conj [half len])))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- distinct-sorted!\\n  \\\"Compact adjacent cmp-equal elements of sorted arr in place; new length.\\\"\\n  [arr cmp]\\n  (let [l (arr/alength arr)]\\n    (if (<= l 1)\\n      l\\n      (loop [w 1\\n             r 1]\\n        (if (== r l)\\n          w\\n          (let [x (arr/aget arr r)]\\n            (if (== 0 (cmp (arr/aget arr (dec w)) x))\\n              (recur w (inc r))\\n              (do (arr/aset arr w x) (recur (inc w) (inc r))))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn from-sorted-array\\n  \\\"Fast path: build a set from a sorted, distinct array (first len items).\\\"\\n  ([cmp arr] (from-sorted-array cmp arr (arr/alength arr) nil))\\n  ([cmp arr len opts]\\n   (let [^Settings sett (make-settings opts)\\n         mx (bf* sett)\\n         avg (quot (+ (min-bf* sett) mx) 2)\\n         leaves (mapv (fn [[from to]]\\n                        (Leaf. (- to from) (arr/asub arr from to) sett))\\n                      (split-ranges len avg mx))]\\n     (loop [level 1\\n            nodes leaves]\\n       (condp == (clojure.core/count nodes)\\n         0 (BTSet. (:meta opts) cmp nil nil (empty-root sett) 0 0 sett nil)\\n         1 (BTSet. (:meta opts) cmp nil nil (nodes 0) len 0 sett nil)\\n         (recur (inc level)\\n                (mapv (fn [[from to]]\\n                        (let [n (- to from)\\n                              ks (arr/anew n)\\n                              cs (arr/anew n)]\\n                          (dotimes [i n]\\n                            (let [c (nodes (+ from i))]\\n                              (arr/aset ks i (node-lim-key c))\\n                              (arr/aset cs i c)))\\n                          (Branch. level n ks cs nil sett)))\\n                      (split-ranges (clojure.core/count nodes) avg mx))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn from-sequential\\n  \\\"Build a set from an arbitrary collection (sorts and dedupes first).\\\"\\n  ([cmp coll] (from-sequential cmp coll nil))\\n  ([cmp coll opts]\\n   (let [arr (arr/from-coll coll)]\\n     (dotimes [i (arr/alength arr)]\\n       (when (nil? (arr/aget arr i)) (nil-element!)))\\n     (arr/asort arr cmp)\\n     (from-sorted-array cmp arr (distinct-sorted! arr cmp) opts))))\\n\\n\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n;; Inspection helpers for the invariant test suite\\n\\n(defn node-keys-vec\\n  \\\"Vector of the node's valid keys [0 ... len).\\\"\\n  [node]\\n  (let [ks (node-keys node)] (mapv #(arr/aget ks %) (range (node-len node)))))\\n\\n\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n;; Durability entry points (§5.1, Phase 4)\\n\\n(defn store-tree\\n  \\\"Store the dirty subgraph, children before parents (Merkle by\\n   construction); returns the root address. No-op returning the existing\\n   address when the set is already stored (§4). An empty set stores as nil\\n   (the existing convention: nil root address, walk of nil => ()).\\\"\\n  [^BTSet s storage]\\n  (when (pos? (.-cnt s)) (-store-tree! s storage)))\\n\\n\\n(defn restore-tree\\n  \\\"A lazy BTSet over `address`; nothing is fetched until traversed. `cnt`\\n   is the element count from the manifest (§5.1 — psset's fault-the-tree\\n   count sentinel is deliberately not ported). nil address => the empty\\n   set (cnt must be 0). The tree adopts the storage's Settings (`-settings`),\\n   which is how the manifest's branching factor reaches every restored\\n   node — never construct the storage with defaults for a tree published\\n   at another branching factor (§5.1).\\\"\\n  [cmp address storage cnt]\\n  (let [sett (-settings storage)]\\n    (if (nil? address)\\n      (BTSet. nil cmp nil storage (empty-root sett) 0 0 sett nil)\\n      (BTSet. nil cmp address storage nil cnt 0 sett nil))))\\n\\n\\n(defn walk-addresses\\n  \\\"Call on-address with every segment key reachable from address, the\\n   root's own address first; descend into a subtree only while on-address\\n   returns truthy (psset's prune protocol — lets a GC mark phase skip\\n   subtrees shared with already-marked versions). nil address => nothing.\\\"\\n  [storage address on-address]\\n  (letfn [(walk-node\\n            [n]\\n            (when (branch? n)\\n              (let [as (node-addresses-arr n)\\n                    l (node-len n)\\n                    deep? (> (node-level n) 1)]\\n                (dotimes [i l]\\n                  (let [a (arr/aget as i)]\\n                    (when (and (on-address a) deep?)\\n                      (walk-node (-restore storage a))))))))]\\n    (when (some? address)\\n      (when (on-address address) (walk-node (-restore storage address))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn clear-test-refs!\\n  \\\"Test seam (§5.3): clear every :test-ref-type reference reachable from\\n   the set's resident nodes (root slot included), so the eviction suite\\n   can force 'collection' deterministically and assert the refault path.\\\"\\n  [^BTSet s]\\n  (letfn [(walk-node\\n            [n]\\n            (when (branch? n)\\n              (when-some [cs (node-children-arr n)]\\n                (dotimes [i (node-len n)]\\n                  (let [slot (arr/aget cs i)]\\n                    (when-some [c (read-ref slot)] (walk-node c))\\n                    (when (instance? TestRef slot) (test-ref-clear! slot)))))))]\\n    (let [slot (raw-root s)]\\n      (when-some [r (read-ref slot)] (walk-node r))\\n      (when (instance? TestRef slot) (test-ref-clear! slot)))))\\n\\n\\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n;; Accessors for storage adapters (dao.data.btree.storage) and tests\\n\\n(defn default-ref-type*\\n  \\\"The per-host default ref-type (§5.3): :soft on the JVM, :strong on\\n   cljs/cljd where :soft would silently degrade to :weak.\\\"\\n  []\\n  default-ref-type)\\n\\n\\n(defn set-address\\n  \\\"The set's own root address; nil while the root is dirty (§4).\\\"\\n  [^BTSet s]\\n  (raw-address s))\\n\\n\\n(defn set-storage\\n  \\\"The set's attached IStorage, or nil for a purely in-memory tree.\\\"\\n  [^BTSet s]\\n  (raw-storage s))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/dao-space/1794057158000-architect-byte-cut-second-review-gpt6astra.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:28:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '804,1138p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc; sed -n '1235,1290p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc; sed -n '1580,1642p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  (node-disj\\n    [this cmp k root? left right sett]\\n    (let [idx0 (search keys len k cmp)\\n          idx (if (neg? idx0) (- (- idx0) 1) idx0)]\\n      (if (== idx len)\\n        UNCHANGED ; not in set\\n        (let [left-child (when (pos? idx) (node-child this (dec idx)))\\n              right-child (when (< idx (dec len)) (node-child this (inc idx)))\\n              lc-len (safe-len left-child)\\n              rc-len (safe-len right-child)\\n              nodes (node-disj (node-child this idx)\\n                               cmp\\n                               k\\n                               false\\n                               left-child\\n                               right-child\\n                               sett)]\\n          (cond\\n            (identical? UNCHANGED nodes) UNCHANGED\\n            (identical? EARLY-EXIT nodes) EARLY-EXIT\\n            :else\\n            (let [n0 (arr/aget nodes 0)\\n                  n1 (arr/aget nodes 1) ; never nil\\n                  n2 (arr/aget nodes 2)\\n                  left-changed? (or (not (identical? left-child n0))\\n                                    (not (== lc-len (safe-len n0))))\\n                  right-changed? (or (not (identical?