{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/agents/malleability.md; rg -n 'measure|pin|slow|scope|nightly|fail|skip|determin|Node|Dart' docs/agents/build-n-test.md | head -n 75; rg --files test/yin/repl | rg '(node|dht|head|store|index|main)'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | sed -n '776,803p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | sed -n '882,924p'; rg -n 'dht|step-owner' src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/interpreter.cljc | head -n 45\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/agents/malleability.md; rg -n 'measure|pin|slow|scope|nightly|fail|skip|determin|Node|Dart' docs/agents/build-n-test.md | head -n 75; rg --files test/yin/repl | rg '(node|dht|head|store|index|main)'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | sed -n '776,803p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | sed -n '882,924p'; rg -n 'dht|step-owner' src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/interpreter.cljc | head -n 45\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: Malleability as evolutionary fitness\ndescription: Code evolution through bounded complexity, loose coupling, and natural selection pressure\ntype: project\n---\n\n# MALLEABILITY: CODE THAT ADAPTS\n\n**Malleability** = how cheaply can code adapt to changing requirements without breaking promises?\n\nThis is the only objective measure of code quality. Requirements always change. Code that adapts survives; code that resists change gets rewritten or abandoned.\n\n## High Cohesion, Loose Coupling\n\n- **High cohesion**: Things that change together should live together.\n- **Loose coupling**: Things that change independently should be isolated.\n\nTightly coupled code requires understanding the entire dependency graph before changing anything. Loosely coupled but cohesive code allows local changes without affecting the rest of the system.\n\n### Measuring Coupling: Dependency Graphs\n\nThis is objective and measurable:\n\n```bash\nclj ./bin/analyze-deps.clj\n```\n\nThis script generates the dependency graph and analyzes it in one step:\n\n**Generates:**\n- `dep-graph.dot` — structured text format describing the dependency graph\n- `dep-graph.svg` — visual SVG representation\n\n**Understanding the Report:**\n\nWhen a namespace is listed as `namespace-name (in:X, out:Y)`:\n- **`in:X`** = X other namespaces depend ON this namespace (incoming edges)\n  - High `in` is good for foundational modules (like `dao.stream`, `yin.vm`)\n  - High `in` means this module is central/widely used\n- **`out:Y`** = this namespace depends ON Y other namespaces (outgoing edges)\n  - Low `out` is better (fewer dependencies = less coupling)\n  - High `out` combined with high `in` is problematic: many things depend on it, but it has many dependencies itself\n\n**Architectural vs. Accidental Coupling:**\nA high `out` count is not automatically a failure. Distinguish between:\n- **Architectural Coupling**: High `out` to *highly stable foundational modules* (e.g., `dao.stream`, `yin.vm`). If the dependencies are bedrock primitives that change rarely, the coupling is stable and necessary for a layered architecture (e.g., the VMs).\n- **Accidental Coupling**: High `out` to *volatile leaf modules* or *unrelated feature sets*. This is a red flag indicating a lack of cohesion and is the primary target for refactoring.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- High `in` + high `out` = many things depend on it, but it's also tightly coupled to many other things. These are candidates for refactoring.\n- High `out` for a leaf module = too many dependencies, extract shared concerns\n\n**Reports:**\n- **Circular dependencies** (A → B → A): bidirectional coupling that prevents modularization\n- **High-degree nodes**: namespaces with 6+ combined incoming/outgoing edges\n- **Strongly connected components**: tightly coupled clusters (candidates for extraction into new namespaces)\n- **Recommendations**: specific actions to break cycles and reduce coupling\n\n**Visual interpretation (in dep-graph.svg):**\n- **Layered graph** (good): Clean separation between modules, minimal cross-connections, clear flow from low-level primitives to high-level features. Layers are visually distinct.\n- **Structural Clustering**: When namespaces have the same outgoing dependencies (like the various VM implementations), they are visually at the same level in the dependency graph. This is another form of clustering: clustering namespaces that share the same dependency graph, indicating a horizontal architectural layer.\n- **Spaghetti graph** (bad): Dense tangles, circular loops, everything connected to everything. Visual chaos reflects architectural decay.\n\nThe graph doesn't lie. Use it to identify where extraction and decoupling are needed.\n\n## Bounded Complexity\n\nIf cohesion and coupling are the principles, **bounded complexity** is the goal.\n\nBounded complexity is a natural consequence of clustering: when you extract dependent code into a namespace and expose only what's necessary at the boundary, you've created a module with bounded complexity.\n\nThe bound is **the interface surface area**: the public API of the namespace.\n\nA small, focused public API (2-5 entry points) bounds complexity. Count public functions (`defn`, not `defn-`). Multiple arities of the same function count as one. Large public APIs mean nothing is isolated. Bounded complexity lets programmers learn code quickly and keeps changes local.\n\n### Public vs. Private: Minimize Interface Surface Area\n\nAny function without external dependencies should be marked private.\n\n**The rule:**\n- Mark a function **private** (`defn-`) if it is called only from within its own namespace.\n- Mark a function **public** (`defn`) only if it is called from other namespaces.\n\nThis keeps the public API surface minimal. Callers see only what they need; internal implementation details stay hidden. The public interface becomes self-documenting: what's exposed is what the namespace is *for*.\n\n**How to verify external callers:**\n\nFor any public function, check if it's called from outside its namespace:\n\n```bash\ngrep -r \"namespace-name/function-name\" src/\n```\n\n**Interpretation:**\n- **0 results**: The function has no external callers. Mark it private (`defn-`) or delete it.\n- **Only matches in its own file**: The function is called only internally. Mark it private (`defn-`).\n- **Matches in other files**: The function has external callers. Keep it public.\n\n**Example:**\n```bash\ngrep -r \"yin.vm/load-program\" src/\n# If this returns results outside yin/vm/*, the function is used externally\n# If only results are in yin/vm/*, or if grep returns nothing, mark it private\n```\n\n**Measure:**\nCount the public functions (see \"Bounded Complexity\" for the 2-5 entry point target). If the count is high, either more functions should be private, or the namespace is doing too many things and needs to be split.\n\n## Workflow: Start Messy, Let Structure Emerge\n\n1. Write everything in one namespace. Don't organize prematurely.\n2. Explore behavior in REPL `(comment ...)` blocks.\n3. Observe which functions depend on each other: call each other, share data shapes, appear together in tests, or are always imported as a unit.\n4. Extract cohesive clusters into separate namespaces when boundaries become clear. Extract them together to keep related changes local.\n5. Delete stale comment blocks ruthlessly.\n6. Iterate as requirements change.\n\n(See \"Selection Pressure\" for testing strategy.)\n\n### When to Extract: Extraction Trigger and Signals\n\n**Trigger**: When a namespace reaches ~12 functions, evaluate whether extraction is needed.\n\n**Signals of a natural cluster**:\n- Functions call each other (high internal coupling)\n- Rarely called from outside (low external coupling)\n- Appear together in tests\n- Work with the same data structures\n- Share the exact same set of external dependencies (structural clustering)\n\n**Extract when**: The structure has already emerged in your code. You're just formalizing what exists, not designing from scratch.\n\n**Naming**: Use problem domain names (`validation`, `auth`, `indexing`), not solution domain (`utils`, `helpers`). See docs/agents/vocabulary.md for vocabulary from biology, economics, physics, philosophy.\n2:description: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n11:bb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\n12:bb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\n13:bb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n15:bb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\n16:bb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\n17:bb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\n19:bb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n20:bb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n21:bb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\n22:bb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n23:bb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n24:# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n25:# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n26:# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n27:# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n28:# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n29:# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n30:# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n31:# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n32:# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n36:# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n39:# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n40:# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n41:# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n43:# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n45:# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n46:# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n49:# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\n50:# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\n53:# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\n59:# for a fix branch, the previously-failing tests green IS the merge bar\n61:# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n65:# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n66:# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n69:npm test             # Node.js tests\n72:# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n73:# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n75:bb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\n76:bb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n77:                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n78:                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n79:                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n102:and spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n130:`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\n132:serially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\n139:Any uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\n140:The final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\n142:failure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\n145:The final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\n146:record is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\n147:`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\n148:`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\n168:| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\n172:set, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\n177:`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\n181:Routing prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\n184:and failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\n187:results, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\n192:`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\n193:serialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\n194:demo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\n197:not (owner ruling 2026-11-04).** A single failing test or one namespace\n199:lane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), the full quick suite, the slow tier,\n200:`bb test:all`, and `nightly.clj` — runs on the remote hosts: the suite has\n201:outgrown the laptop. The slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM\n211:recorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\n220:`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\n222:reuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\n225:Stamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\n229:`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\n231:does not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\n236:both completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\n253:bb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\n254:bb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\ntest/yin/repl/index_test.cljc\ntest/yin/repl/dht_head_test.cljc\ntest/yin/repl/ast_index_test.cljc\ntest/yin/repl/main_test.cljc\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc\ntest/yin/repl/host/node_test.cljs\ntest/yin/repl/store_test.cljc\n   776\t(defn- members-bytes\n   777\t  \"Encodes [key value] pairs (value nil for set elements), members at depth d,\n   778\t   refusing duplicate canonical encodings (dup-class) and portable-equality\n   779\t   collapses before the collection is built.\n   780\t   Returns [[key-bytes key-bytes-hex value-bytes-or-nil] ...].\"\n   781\t  [pairs set? dup-class d]\n   782\t  (let [wired (mapv (fn [[k v]]\n   783\t                      (let [kb (value-bytes k d)\n   784\t                            vb (when-not set? (value-bytes v d))]\n   785\t                        [kb (bytes-hex kb) vb]))\n   786\t                    pairs)]\n   787\t    (when (< (count (set (map second wired))) (count wired))\n   788\t      (refuse dup-class))\n   789\t    (when (collapse? (map first pairs))\n   790\t      (refuse :equality-collapse))\n   791\t    wired))\n   792\t\n   793\t\n   794\t(defn- map-bytes\n   795\t  \"A map at depth d; keys and values one level deeper.\"\n   796\t  [m d]\n   797\t  (deep! d :unsupported-value)\n   798\t  (let [wired (members-bytes (seq m) false :duplicate-key (inc d))\n   799\t        sorted (sort #(compare-canonical-bytes (first %1) (first %2)) wired)\n   800\t        pair-buffers (mapcat (fn [[kb _ vb]] [kb vb]) sorted)]\n   801\t    (concat-buffers (cons (write-head 5 (count wired)) pair-buffers))))\n   802\t\n   803\t\n   882\t(defn- value-bytes\n   883\t  \"The canonical CBOR bytes of one value whose outermost item sits at depth d,\n   884\t   or a refusal.\"\n   885\t  [x d]\n   886\t  (cond\n   887\t    (nil? x) (do (deep! d :unsupported-value) (bytes/from-vector [0xf6]))\n   888\t    (boolean? x) (do (deep! d :unsupported-value) (bytes/from-vector [(if x 0xf5 0xf4)]))\n   889\t    (string? x) (do (deep! d :unsupported-value)\n   890\t                    (let [ub (host text/utf8-encode (check-text x))]\n   891\t                      (concat-buffers [(write-head 3 (bytes/length ub)) ub])))\n   892\t    (keyword? x) (identifier-bytes \"dao.jing/keyword\" x d)\n   893\t    (symbol? x) (meta-bytes x d #(identifier-bytes \"dao.jing/symbol\" x %))\n   894\t    (byte-payload? x) (do (deep! d :unsupported-value)\n   895\t                          (let [len (bytes/length x)\n   896\t                                head (write-head 2 len)\n   897\t                                out (bytes/alloc (+ (bytes/length head) len))]\n   898\t                            (bytes/copy! head 0 out 0 (bytes/length head))\n   899\t                            (bytes/copy! x 0 out (bytes/length head) len)\n   900\t                            out))\n   901\t    (numeric? x) (number-bytes x d)\n   902\t    (record? x) (refuse :unsupported-value \"record\")\n   903\t    (map? x) (meta-bytes x d #(map-bytes x %))\n   904\t    (vector? x) (meta-bytes x d (fn [d]\n   905\t                                  (deep! d :unsupported-value)\n   906\t                                  (let [items (mapv #(value-bytes % (inc d)) x)]\n   907\t                                    (concat-buffers (cons (write-head 4 (count items)) items)))))\n   908\t    (set? x) (meta-bytes x d #(set-bytes x %))\n   909\t    (or (list? x) (seq? x)) (meta-bytes x d #(list-bytes x %))\n   910\t    :else (refuse :unsupported-value (type-name x))))\n   911\t\n   912\t\n   913\t(defn encode\n   914\t  \"The canonical CBOR payload bytes of one supported value (byte[] on the\n   915\t   JVM, Uint8Array on JavaScript, Uint8List on Dart). Refuses unsupported\n   916\t   values, unpaired surrogates, nesting deeper than max-depth, and\n   917\t   collections whose members collide after normalization.\"\n   918\t  [value]\n   919\t  (value-bytes value 1))\n   920\t\n   921\t\n   922\t(defn copy-bytes\n   923\t  \"A fresh copy of host byte array bs, so a holder's snapshot cannot be\n   924\t   changed through the array a caller keeps.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:53:   `(require 'dao.space.dht)` and `(require 'yin.link)` activate two more\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:55:   the plain functions of `dao.space.dht` and `yin.vm.linker.dht`\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:56:   (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 10): each answer checks its arguments, makes one\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:60:   (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 6), each one call into\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:66:            [dao.space.dht :as dht]\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:73:            [yin.repl.dht :as repl.dht]\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:78:            [yin.vm.linker.dht :as linker.dht]\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:93:(def dht-module-name\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:94:  \"The module `(require 'dao.space.dht)` activates: the plain Clojure DHT\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:95:   path (`dao.space.dht`) as host functions over the shell's own DHT node.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:96:  'dao.space.dht)\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:99:(def dht-ops\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:100:  \"The `dao.stream.apply` operations the `dao.space.dht` module requests:\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:102:   `dao.space.dht` function of that name, `retry` and `cancel`,\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:104:   `module-status`, answered by the `yin.vm.linker.dht` function of that\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:105:   name (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 10).\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:106:  {'load-index ::dht-load-index\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:107:   'load-status ::dht-load-status\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:108:   'q ::dht-q\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:109:   'retry ::dht-retry\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:110:   'cancel ::dht-cancel\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:111:   'load-module ::dht-load-module\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:112:   'module-status ::dht-module-status})\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:118:   (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 10), over the shell's own DHT node.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:124:   `publish`, answered by `yin.vm.linker.dht/publish-name!`, and `names`,\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:125:   answered by `yin.vm.linker.dht/names`.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:132:   the moved names (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.6, 5.7), each one call\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:177:(defn- dht-export\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:178:  \"A `dao.space.dht` export: a data constructor whose effect parks the\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:179:   caller on the call pair under the operation `dht-ops` names.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:182:    (module/make-effect ::call {:op (get dht-ops sym), :args (vec args)})))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:185:(def ^:private dht-arities\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:190:(defn activate-dht\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:191:  \"`registry` with the `dao.space.dht` host module installed.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:194:    registry dht-module-name\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:195:    (into {} (map (fn [sym] [sym (dht-export sym)])) (keys dht-ops))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:199:          dht-arities)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:230:   dht-module-name activate-dht\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:237:   require of `dao.space.query` or `dao.space.dht` installs that module\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:654:      (and (= :loaded (:status status)) (= dht/index-kind (:kind status)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:660:   `(ok node')` answers; a `:dao.space.dht/refused` of any code is\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:665:         (if-let [code (:dao.space.dht/refused (ex-data e))]\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:677:(defn- dht-answer\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:678:  \"The response to one `dao.space.dht` call, answered from `node` — the\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:679:   shell's DHT node — by the `dao.space.dht` function the operation\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"agent_message","text":"The dependency is broader than the 1,139-line module. `yin.repl` also carries DHT state through linker rollback and filters DHT events; `yin.repl.query` imports DHT implementations directly. The refactor gate therefore needs to check the engine’s dependency closure, not just the moved file. Also, the “eight distinct” assertion counts manifest addresses, not acknowledgement timestamps—loosening timing expectations would miss the failure."}}
